; Planner.scaly - Generic resolution, type inference, and name mangling.
; Transforms Model (generics) into Plan (concrete types, mangled names).
;
; Ported afresh from scalyc/Planner.cpp (~13K LOC, ~97 methods).
; Covers the full planning pipeline — plan_program → plan_module →
; plan_namespace/plan_concept → plan_structure/plan_union/plan_global
; /plan_function/plan_operator/plan_initializer/plan_deinitializer —
; HM type inference (unify, solve_constraints, apply_substitution),
; Itanium ABI name mangling, overload resolution with generic
; inference, member-access + method-chain resolution, iterator
; protocol, and branch-result unification.
;
; The only paths that abort via not_implemented are the package
; loader's parse+model steps, which need Scaly-side access to the
; Parser and Modeler as callable modules.

; scaly.io imports are load-bearing beyond visibility: the use alias
; feeds the dotted-call extern-decl gate (typed declarations for
; cross-package File./Path. calls instead of varargs fallbacks).
use scaly.os.File
use scaly.os.Path

use scalyc.compiler.ConceptIndex.ConceptIndex
use scalyc.compiler.FunctionIndex.FunctionIndex
use scalyc.compiler.Interface.InterfaceFact
use scalyc.compiler.Model.*
use scalyc.compiler.Plan.*

; libc extern for loud-failing stubs.
function exit(code: i32) extern

; ============================================================================
; Stack guard for the recursive descent (2026-08-04).
;
; The planner recurses once per NESTING level of a construct, NOT per function
; and NOT per call-graph edge — measured 2026-08-04 with the three probe shapes
; (800 independent functions: 256 KB; an 800-deep call chain f0->f1->...: 256 KB,
; i.e. planning a call does NOT recurse into the callee; an 800-level nested
; expression: 16 MB). So a growing codebase cannot exhaust the stack, but ONE
; deeply nested function can, and the practical driver in this tree is the long
; `else if` chain: it nests one level per arm, and each arm costs ~15 KB with the
; opt -O2 compiler and ~12x that with the UNOPTIMIZED bootstrap stages. dazzle's
; TeXFOTBuilder.scaly has a 126-arm chain — that single chain is what makes
; /tmp/scalyc_stage2 need ~24 MB to compile the package (CLAUDE.md iron rule).
;
; Without a guard the failure is a bare SIGSEGV with ONE frame in the lldb
; backtrace (the guard page) and no file, line, or hint — an hour of looking in
; the wrong place, twice now. The live limit is what the guard reads, so it
; adapts to whatever `ulimit -s` the run actually has instead of hard-coding a
; nesting count that would be either unsafe at 8 MB or wrong at 64 MB.
;
; ★It goes through a SHIM since 2026-08-10 (stage 7, brocken 5), where it used
; to be a direct `getrlimit(RLIMIT_STACK)` extern with a local two-u64 struct.
; Windows has no getrlimit, and the difference is not just the spelling: POSIX
; answers with a PROCESS resource limit, Windows with the CURRENT THREAD's
; bounds (GetCurrentThreadStackLimits). "How big is the stack I am running on"
; is the question this caller actually has, and it is the one that survives the
; translation — so the shim exports that question rather than either platform's
; answer to a different one. 0 means "cannot tell" and takes the fallback,
; which also absorbs the old RLIM_INFINITY case. This is the one place the
; Windows port reaches into the COMPILER rather than the runtime substrate.
function scaly_stack_limit() returns size_t extern
function getenv(name: pointer[const_char]) returns pointer[const_char] extern
; atoll, not atol: `long atol(const char*)` is 32-bit on Win64 (LLP64) while
; the one committed seed declares this once for every target, so a budget above
; 2 GB would truncate or flip sign there. atoll is C99 and present on MSVC too,
; so no shim is needed — see check 4 of tests/abi/run.sh.
function atoll(s: pointer[const_char]) returns i64 extern

; ============================================================================
; Scope / binding machinery.
;
; Defined at module scope so callers outside `define Planner` can construct
; them (scope state will eventually live inside the Planner struct; for now
; every scope-aware helper takes the stack as an explicit parameter).
; ============================================================================

; One local variable's entry. is_on_page tracks RBMM lifetime — true for
; values allocated with $, #, or ^name so the code generator can emit
; appropriate page-cleanup hooks.
define LocalBinding
(
    ; `var`: a local that is handed a reference to something frame-local
    ; after its declaration is re-stamped -- stamp_local_ref_holder#.
    var binding_type: scalyc.compiler.Plan.PlannedType
    is_mutable: bool
    is_on_page: bool
    is_parameter: bool
    ; Set only on the shadow a null test pushes -- see define_narrowed#.
    ; The binding's DECLARED type is still the Option, so the author's `*x`
    ; remains an honest deref and report_deref_of_ref# must let it through.
    var is_narrowed: bool
    ; A `when` binding: a COPY of the union arm (report_when_binding_store#).
    var is_when_copy: bool
)

; One HARD diagnostic: a construct the planner could not resolve and that
; would otherwise be silently dropped from the emitted IR. `start` is the
; byte offset of the construct in its source file (Span.start); cli.run
; formats it as file:line:col like stage-0's formatErrorHeader and fails
; the compile.
define PlannerDiagnostic
(
    start: size_t
    message: String
    ; Source file the `start` byte offset is relative to. Multi-file
    ; compiles put each module's nodes in their own file's coordinate
    ; space, so a diagnostic must carry its file or cli.run formats the
    ; offset against the wrong (entry) file. Mirrors stage-0's per-error
    ; File capture (Planner.cpp planModule's `File = Mod.File`).
    file: String
)

; Escape checker (RBMM blocker C), Vector 3: pass-to-storing-callee.
; One summary entry: the function at instantiated_functions[callee_fn_index]
; STORES its parameter #param_index into frame-external memory. dest_kind 0 =
; the destination is that callee's parameter #dest_index (e.g. `this.field`);
; dest_kind 1 = the destination is a module global or a pointer deref (@ext,
; always frame-external). callee_param_count is the callee's Input length, used
; to align a call's args from the right (skipping any leading page arg).
; Indices are computed at collect time so the per-call check needs no signature
; lookup. Mirrors Planner.cpp's EscapeSummary/CalleeParamNames.
; A per-parameter persist fact: "callee_fn_index's function stores its
; parameter `param_index`, accessed at the k-limited FIELD path
; (src_path_len / src_field0 / src_field1), into frame-external memory
; (dest_kind 1 = global/deref, dest_kind 0 = into callee param dest_index's
; field)". The field path is encoded as fixed scalars rather than a
; Vector[String] (Design-B spec's illustrative form): k is fixed at 2, and
; struct field INDICES — not names — align exactly between the callee's
; store site and the caller's construction (both reference the same struct
; type, so a field has one stable index), while staying pure scalars that
; copy trivially by value in Array[EscapeRecord] with no String/RBMM
; lifetime hazard. src_path_len 0 = the whole parameter (byte-compatible
; with every pre-field-reshape record); 1 = param.<field0>; 2 =
; param.<field0>.<field1>. A path longer than 2 is truncated to 2 (a
; truncated match is treated as "this field subtree", resolved SAFE unless
; provably local — see region_of_arg_path#).
define EscapeRecord
(
    callee_fn_index: size_t
    callee_param_count: size_t
    param_index: size_t
    dest_kind: size_t
    dest_index: size_t
    src_path_len: size_t
    src_field0: size_t
    src_field1: size_t
)

; Result of resolving a stored/forwarded operand back to a (parameter, field
; path) pair — the access-path generalization of escape_value_param_root.
; param_index -1 = not rooted at a parameter/alias. All scalars (k=2 field
; path), so it returns/copies by value with no RBMM lifetime concern.
define EscapeAccess
(
    param_index: int
    path_len: size_t
    field0: size_t
    field1: size_t
)

; which inputs a body hands back WHOLE — the parameter itself, not a copy
; of its elements (`new_type_list(types)` returns a fresh list holding
; types' members): `cur` for the body being scanned, `by_name` parallel to
; Planner.deep_returns_by_name. Behind a reference: the Planner record is full.
; A hash index over a name table the deep gate asks by name (`deep_by_name`,
; `deep_returns_by_name`, `deep_qualified_all`): name -> its FIRST position.
; The tables were scanned linearly per lookup, and the armed gate spent 3.5x
; the plan time of the tscaly root in those scans (2026-09-23, `sample`).
; The table doubles past half load and is refilled from the array it mirrors.
define GrowIndex (var table: ConceptIndex  var count: size_t)

; unit_variant_owner#'s index: payload-less variant name -> the position in
; `concepts` of the ONE non-generic union declaring it, or UV_AMBIGUOUS when
; two differently named unions do. `indexed` is how much of `concepts` it has
; seen: the registry only grows, so each ask first enters what arrived since.
; Before it, every bare-name lookup scanned every union and every variant --
; 31 % of the tscaly root's plan time (2026-09-26, `sample`).
define UnitVariantIndex (var table: ConceptIndex  var count: size_t  var indexed: size_t)
define UV_AMBIGUOUS size_t 0x4000000000000000

; A name -> FIRST position table over a list that only grows, entered at the
; list's one insertion site (`keys`/`poss` in entry order, for the rebuild
; past half load). It answers what the linear scan it replaces answered: the
; first entry of that name.
define NameIndex
@resident true @inplace true (
    var table: ConceptIndex
    keys: Array[String]
    poss: Array[size_t]
)

; Method tables per member list (collect_methods_in#, member_list_store_mask#):
; a list is known by its buffer's address -> `map_keys`/`map_vals` (open
; addressing, 0 = empty) -> its table number; table k maps a method name to
; the first member position (+1) of that name, `next[bases[k] + pos]` to the
; next (+1, 0 ends), in MEMBER ORDER. Built at a list's first ask; the Model's
; member lists never change. Both scans compared every member's name per ask
; -- ~16 % of the tscaly root's plan time, whose Checker alone carries
; thousands of methods (2026-09-26, `sample`).
define MemberIndex
@resident true @inplace true (
    var cap: size_t
    var used: size_t
    map_keys: Array[u64]
    map_vals: Array[size_t]
    tables: Array[ConceptIndex]
    bases: Array[size_t]
    next: Array[size_t]
)

; The escape summary by callee: mangled name -> a chain of positions in
; `escape_records`, in insertion order (`heads`/`tails` per chain, `next` per
; record, positions stored +1 so 0 ends a chain; `names` per chain for the
; rebuild past half load). Every call used to test EVERY record's callee
; name -- ~15 % of the tscaly root's plan time (2026-09-26, `sample`).
define EscIndex
@resident true @inplace true (
    var table: ConceptIndex
    names: Array[String]
    heads: Array[size_t]
    tails: Array[size_t]
    next: Array[size_t]
)

; the residence table's two lookups (return_residence_index#: name and span;
; return_residence_static#: owner's last segment and name, `dup` marking a key
; two functions share — the linear scan answered 0 for those), built once
; after build_return_residence_set# fills the table
define ResidenceIndex (var by_key: ConceptIndex  var by_static: ConceptIndex  var static_dup: ConceptIndex)

define DeepIndexes
(
    res: ref[ResidenceIndex]
    by_name: ref[GrowIndex]
    ret_names: ref[GrowIndex]
    ; the LAST dotted segment of every deep_returns_by_name entry
    ; (persist_callee_named#: `seg` itself or any `Owner.seg`)
    returns_seg: ref[GrowIndex]
    qualified: ref[GrowIndex]
)

; A body's residence class across a SELF call (`return this.f(x.sub)`): the
; call asks the body's own, still undecided class and read 0, so every
; recursive function stayed unknown. `mode` 1 reads the self call as neutral
; (class 4, which decides nothing in the merge), `mode` 2 maps it through the
; candidate `cls`/`arg` — iterated from that bottom until the class stops
; moving, the least fixpoint. `takes_this` and `arity` recognise the call.
define RrSelf
(
    var mode: int
    var name: String
    var takes_this: bool
    var arity: size_t
    var cls: size_t
    var arg: size_t
    var hit: bool
)

; `seen` marks an entry noted at least once (the first note replaces the
; all-ones default instead of OR-ing into it).
define DeepRetWhole (var cur: u64  by_name: ref[Array[u64]]  seen: ref[Array[bool]])

define Planner
{
    ; ========================================================================
    ; Not-yet-ported marker.
    ;
    ; Every stub in this file funnels through here. Prints the method name
    ; to stderr and exits with code 111 so a caller that touches unported
    ; logic gets a precise, visible failure (not a silent null).
    ; ========================================================================

    procedure not_implemented(method_name: Slice[char]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns int io linked

    ; Typed unreachable shim for functions that return PlannedOperand.
    ; The exit() in not_implemented terminates the process, so the dummy
    ; PlannedOperand below is never observed — it exists only so Scaly's
    ; type checker accepts the call site.
    procedure not_implemented_operand(method_name: Slice[char]) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true @deep true returns PlannedOperand io linked

    ; ========================================================================
    ; Planner - the per-compilation-unit state.
    ;
    ; Mirrors the private fields of scalyc/Planner.h: scope stack, type
    ; substitutions, HM constraints + solution, concept registry,
    ; instantiation caches, function/operator registries, package
    ; search paths, loaded-package cache, and error accumulator.
    ; ========================================================================

    ; DeepGateState: the deep escape gate (SCALYC_DEEP_GATE): per-function persist masks, the
    ; gate tables by name and by mangled name, and the indexes over them.
    ; Held by the Planner behind `gate`; allocated on its page in its init.
    define DeepGateState
    @resident true @inplace true (
        var deep_persist_mask: u64
        var deep_this_mask: u64
        var deep_whole_mask: u64
        ; positions whose PAGE the body takes (`Page.get(p)`, `^p` on a
        ; page parameter) to allocate on: the argument must be page-hosted
        var deep_pageof_mask: u64
        deep_state_pageof: Array[size_t]
        deep_gate_pageof: ref[Array[u64]]
        ; INTO WHICH parameter: slot k (1..15) holds the positions kept in
        ; parameter k's guts (slot 0 is deep_this_mask); a position 16 or
        ; beyond counts as beyond. Per body, per name entry and per gate
        ; entry, sixteen slots each, flattened
        deep_into_masks: Array[u64]
        deep_by_name_into: Array[u64]
        deep_state_into: Array[size_t]
        deep_state_hasinto: ref[Array[size_t]]
        deep_gate_into: ref[Array[u64]]
        deep_by_name_this: Array[u64]
        deep_by_name_beyond: Array[u64]
        deep_by_name_whole: Array[u64]
        deep_by_name_pageof: Array[u64]
        deep_qualified_all: Array[String]
        ; the owners of the bodies this root plans; a known owner that is NOT
        ; among them lives in another package (its body linked)
        deep_scanned_owners: ref[Array[String]]
        ; --emit-interface: run the deep pre-pass without judging, so a
        ; package's interface can state each routine's deep masks (`@deep`)
        var deep_facts: bool
        deep_state_this: Array[size_t]
        deep_state_beyond: Array[size_t]
        deep_state_whole: Array[size_t]
        deep_by_name_mask: Array[u64]
        ; the deep verdicts (host_census#): every planned body is recorded
        ; under --host-report (deep_fns/deep_mangled) and judged to a
        ; fixpoint, because the question is TRANSITIVE — a body that hands a
        ; parameter to a persisting callee persists it too. deep_by_name is
        ; the persisting callees by plain name, what a Model-level call can
        ; be matched against; persist_deep_names/vals is the census's lookup
        ; by mangled name.
        deep_fns: Array[Function]
        deep_mangled: Array[String]
        deep_state: Array[size_t]
        deep_by_name: Array[String]
        ; the second verdict: a body whose RETURN carries a parameter (or a
        ; call result that does) hands the caller a value holding it —
        ; deep_ret_state per body, deep_returns_by_name for the Model-level
        ; match; persist_ret_hit is the scan's own flag
        deep_ret_state: Array[size_t]
        ; per name in deep_returns_by_name, WHICH inputs the body hands back
        ; (bit 0 is `this`); deep_ret_state holds the same mask per body.
        ; Behind a reference: the Planner's page budget.
        deep_returns_masks: ref[Array[u64]]
        deep_ret_whole: ref[DeepRetWhole]
        deep_idx: ref[DeepIndexes]
        var deep_ret_mask: u64
        deep_returns_by_name: Array[String]
        deep_returns_mangled: Array[String]
        ; the escape gate asks the DEEP persist verdict (deep_state, indexed
        ; like rr_fns) instead of the shallow scan — armed by default since
        ; 2026-09-23, SCALYC_DEEP_GATE_OFF=1 restores the shallow scan
        var deep_gate: bool
        var deep_trace: bool
        ; the gate's post-pass table: per planned body (mangled) the deep
        ; mask and its parameter count, filled at plan_function#
        deep_gate_mangled: Array[String]
        deep_gate_masks: Array[u64]
        deep_gate_this: Array[u64]
        deep_gate_beyond: Array[u64]
        deep_gate_whole: Array[u64]
        deep_gate_plen: Array[size_t]
        deep_gate_takes_this: Array[bool]
        var deep_gate_index: ConceptIndex
    )
    {
        init() linked
    }

    ; ResidenceState: return residence (build_return_residence_set#): what each call result is
    ; built on — the per-function buckets, locals, parameters and classes.
    ; Held by the Planner behind `residence`; allocated on its page in its init.
    define ResidenceState
    @resident true @inplace true (
        rr_self: ref[RrSelf]
        ; Return residence (build_return_residence_set#): per function, keyed
        ; like return_page_names, rr_fns the Model functions in the same
        ; order; the name buckets answer a Model-level call; rr_* below is
        ; the classifier's scratch; rr_armed (SCALYC_RR) turns the call-site
        ; stamp on.
        rr_fns: Array[Function]
        rr_bucket_names: Array[String]
        rr_bucket_class: Array[size_t]
        rr_bucket_arg: Array[size_t]
        rr_bucket_conflict: Array[size_t]
        rr_bucket_this: Array[size_t]
        ; 1 when every function of the name returns a scalar (or nothing)
        rr_bucket_scalar: Array[size_t]
        var rr_bucket_index: ConceptIndex
        var rr_lookup_arg: size_t
        var rr_lookup_this: size_t
        var rr_trace: bool
        rr_tainted: Array[String]
        ; locals bound with an UNKNOWN class (0: a callee the fixpoint has not
        ; decided yet) — an expression over one answers 0, never 1, so the body
        ; stays open for the next round; before, round one closed it at class 1
        ; for good (dazzle's attribute_string, whose `value` callee was decided
        ; one round later). Behind a reference: the Planner's page budget.
        rr_unknown: ref[Array[String]]
        rr_local_names: Array[String]
        rr_local_class: Array[size_t]
        rr_local_arg: Array[size_t]
        rr_local_concept: Array[String]
        rr_param_names: Array[String]
        rr_param_page: Array[size_t]
        rr_param_scalar: Array[size_t]
        rr_param_concept: Array[String]
        var rr_owner: String
        var rr_result_class: size_t
        var rr_result_arg: size_t
        var rr_result_seen: bool
        var rr_result_conflict: bool
        var rr_class_arg: size_t
        var rr_static_arg: size_t
        var rr_armed: bool
        rr_local_page_names: Array[String]
        rr_local_page_of: Array[int]
        ; every planned body's return class and argument, by mangled name,
        ; for the post-pass by-value judge (rr_class_of_call#)
        var rr_class_index: ConceptIndex
        rr_class_vals: Array[size_t]
        rr_class_args: Array[size_t]
    )
    {
        init() linked
    }

    ; PersistState: the persist analysis: taint, masks, aliases and the per-function summaries
    ; the deep gate and the promotion read.
    ; Held by the Planner behind `persistence`; allocated on its page in its init.
    define PersistState
    @resident true @inplace true (
        ; --host-report census (host_census#): the named-page constructions
        ; (`T^host(...)`, `T^this(...)`, any `^name`) whose value never leaves
        ; the routine that builds them. escape_census switches the checker's
        ; walk into census mode: region_of_operand answers LOCAL for the
        ; candidate (census_box_start pins an unbound one by its span) and its
        ; aliases (census_names), the diagnostics stay silent and escape_found
        ; is read as the verdict. census_escaped is the inline half's verdict.
        ; The report is three parallel arrays for cli.print_located.
        ; Persist scan (function_persists_param_guts#): the names a
        ; parameter's guts may travel under inside one body (the parameters
        ; and every local that received one), and the body's locals — both
        ; on the Planner's page, because the walk grows them from nested
        ; blocks and a frame-hosted Array's growth would die with the frame.
        persist_tainted: Array[String]
        ; the ORIGIN MASK of every tainted name: bit i = derived from input
        ; parameter i (bit 63 = beyond); a scan's verdict is the union of the
        ; masks that reached a persisting store — per PARAMETER, so the gate
        ; can ask which argument positions persist
        persist_tmask: Array[u64]
        var persist_last_mask: u64
        ; the split the post-pass needs: which positions persist INTO THE
        ; RECEIVER (`this`, judged against the receiver argument at the call
        ; site), and which are persisted WHOLE (a bare name) rather than as a
        ; FIELD of the parameter (judged through the field-points-to map)
        persist_twhole: Array[u64]
        var persist_last_whole: u64
        ; per parameter: its ONE generic argument is a scalar (`ref[int]`,
        ; `Slice[char]`, `Vector[u8]`) — what is stored through or into it
        ; carries no reference
        persist_pelem_scalar: Array[bool]
        ; locals that ALIAS a parameter's guts (`let a this.need_aux()`,
        ; `let e p.entry`): a store into them lands in that parameter
        persist_alias_names: Array[String]
        persist_alias_dest: Array[int]
        var persist_masked: bool
        persist_locals: Array[String]
        ; the parameters' names and concepts, for the scalar-field question
        persist_pnames: Array[String]
        persist_pconcepts: Array[String]
        var persist_ret_hit: bool
        persist_deep_names: Array[String]
        persist_deep_vals: Array[size_t]
        ; Phase-5 class 1 (value-guts persist summary memo): mangled name ->
        ; does the function's body store a parameter's VALUE into state that
        ; outlives its frame (this-field/global putadd of a bare param, or a
        ; param embedded in a bare-lifetime construction —
        ; `errors.add message`, `diagnostics.add(PlannerDiagnostic(...,
        ; message, ...))`)? Consulted at call resolution ONLY when an
        ; argument's planned type carries Local life (a Design-B
        ; local-flipped call result), so the model walk runs rarely and the
        ; memo stays small. Planner-lived, append-only, NEVER truncated —
        ; summaries are global facts about function bodies, not per-function
        ; scan state (the s02i base-scope trap does not apply). Declared
        ; LAST so the field indices of everything before stay stable.
        persist_summary_names: Array[String]
        persist_summary_vals: Array[size_t]
    )
    {
        init() linked
    }

    define Planner
    @resident true @inplace true (
        var file: String

        ; Scope stack — stored as one flat Array of bindings with a
        ; parallel Array of scope boundaries (indices where each scope
        ; begins). This avoids the Array-of-Array memory-model problem
        ; where a nested Array would lose its `Page.get(this)` anchor
        ; after being copied into the outer Array's exclusive page.
        ;
        ; push_scope    → boundaries.add(bindings.length)
        ; pop_scope     → bindings.length = last boundary, drop boundary
        ; define_local  → bindings.add(...)
        ; lookup_local  → reverse linear scan of bindings
        scope_bindings: Array[KeyValuePair[String, LocalBinding]]
        scope_boundaries: Array[size_t]

        ; Generic parameter substitutions active while planning a
        ; specific struct/function instantiation. Name-keyed
        ; (e.g. "T" → PlannedType for int).
        type_substitutions: Array[KeyValuePair[String, PlannedType]]

        ; Fresh type-variable id counter for the Hindley-Milner
        ; constraint solver; see fresh_type_var below.
        var next_type_var_id: size_t

        ; Pending constraints (grow as the planner encounters equalities
        ; it can't resolve yet) and the accumulated solution from the
        ; most recent solve_and_apply call.
        constraints: Array[TypeConstraint]
        solution: Array[KeyValuePair[size_t, PlannedType]]

        ; Concept registry — maps a readable name (e.g. "Vector") to the
        ; Model.Concept that owns it. Populated by register_concept; used
        ; by lookup_concept during type resolution and method dispatch.
        concepts: Array[KeyValuePair[String, Concept]]

        ; O(1) name -> index map over `concepts`, maintained in lockstep at the
        ; single register_concept insertion site. Replaces lookup_concept's
        ; backward linear scan (the hottest planner function). Stores indices,
        ; not pointers, because `concepts` reallocates as it grows. Concrete,
        ; malloc'd, open-addressing — sidesteps the s188 RBMM-HashMap bug.
        concept_index: ConceptIndex
        unit_variants: ref[UnitVariantIndex]

        ; Instantiation caches. Every PlannedStructure / PlannedUnion /
        ; PlannedFunction / PlannedGlobal a call into the planner
        ; produces is appended here so the final Plan can carry the
        ; complete list for emission.
        instantiated_structures: Array[PlannedStructure]
        instantiated_unions: Array[scalyc.compiler.Plan.PlannedUnion]
        ; Names of every `delegate` (function-pointer) concept registered.
        ; Flows to Plan.delegates so the emitter's map_type lowers a
        ; delegate-typed value to an LLVM ptr (GAP B). Empty for the scaly
        ; package (no delegates) → map_type byte-identical, fixpoint-safe.
        delegate_names: Array[String]
        ; TYPE ALIASES (`define LLVMValueRef pointer[LLVMOpaqueValue]`, modelled
        ; in Modeler.scaly's ConstantSyntax branch with no operation as a
        ; Concept whose Definition is a Type). plan_type# resolves an alias to
        ; its target so NO later pass ever sees the alias name -- see the block
        ; there for why resolving downstream instead is not an option.
        ; ★This flag is what makes the resolution FREE where no alias exists:
        ; plan_type# runs on every written type in the program, and an
        ; unconditional concept lookup there would be paid by every compile.
        ; False for a tree with no aliases => plan_type# is byte-identical.
        var has_type_aliases: bool
        ; Recursion bound for an alias CHAIN (`define A B` / `define B A`),
        ; threaded as a field rather than a parameter only because plan_type#
        ; has ~500 call sites; 16 is far past any honest chain.
        var alias_depth: int
        ; The enclosing function's planned return type during body planning
        ; (GAP B): a lambda returned where this is a delegate type becomes an
        ; ESCAPING closure (env on the caller page `#`). Null outside a function
        ; body or when the function has no declared return type.
        var current_return_type: ref[PlannedType]?
        ; The body being planned is an uninstantiated generic TEMPLATE (a type
        ; parameter was seeded with a fresh type variable, plan_function#): its
        ; calls on a `T` do not resolve, and every instantiation is planned
        ; separately and checked (report_effect_free_statement#).
        var planning_template: bool
        ; Depth of generic FREE function instantiations under way
        ; (instantiate_generic_function#): their bodies see every concept, as
        ; a generic concept's do (use_vis_check#) -- a type argument is a
        ; concept of the CALLER's package the template's file never names.
        var instantiating_functions: size_t
        ; The package directory of the root being compiled (package_dir_of#
        ; of its main module's file), "" outside packages/: a constant of
        ; another package is planned on demand (plan_foreign_constant#).
        var root_package: String
        ; Names bound to a stack (non-escaping, Lifetime.Local) closure in the
        ; current function (GAP B escape checking). Returning one is an escape
        ; (the record lives on this frame) — flagged as a hard diagnostic.
        ; Truncated to its entry length at each function boundary.
        local_closure_names: Array[String]
        ; Page inference (let-bound box escape): names bound to a `&^this`/`&^rp`
        ; box (`let r &^this <call>`). The box places its value on a SURVIVING
        ; page (the borrowed receiver / caller rp), so such a local may be
        ; returned/stored/passed even though its binding_type carries the boxed
        ; value's stale Local inner. region_of_operand# ranks a use of one Caller.
        ; Covers the bind-then-escape shape where intervening statements (e.g. a
        ; type_substitutions restore) prevent inlining the box into the sink.
        ; Truncated per function boundary.
        local_box_names: Array[String]
        ; Page inference, gap 1 (backward binding promotion). escaping_return_
        ; locals: names a pre-scan found returned bare (`return x`) somewhere in
        ; the current function body — their `let x <alloc>` binding's allocation
        ; must live on the CALLER's page, not the local page. promoted_binding_
        ; names: the subset actually promoted at the binding (init was a
        ; relocatable allocation), consulted at the return site to skip the
        ; escape diagnostic. Both truncated at each function boundary.
        escaping_return_locals: Array[String]
        ; the function whose returns are being pre-scanned returns a VIEW
        ; (`Slice[T]`) -- collect_view_receiver#
        var scan_returns_view: bool
        ; the locals collect_view_receiver# found (and nothing else did):
        ; a view-typed one among them is not promoted
        view_receiver_locals: Array[String]
        ; locals RETURNED bare by a view-returning function: their
        ; initializer is the view (collect_view_sources#)
        view_alias_locals: Array[String]
        promoted_binding_names: Array[String]
        ; lambda literals planned as OPERANDS, so a call can turn one into a
        ; closure for a delegate parameter (closure_for_lambda_arg#)
        lambda_arg_models: Array[Lambda]
        lambda_arg_starts: Array[size_t]
        ; Hole-1 field provenance (escape soundness): names of frame-local
        ; VALUE-struct bindings constructed with a Local-ref argument
        ; (`let h Holder(&b)` where &b points into this frame). A later
        ; `return h.field` / store / pass of a reference-like field of such a
        ; binding hands out a frame address -> rejected. Coarse (taints the
        ; whole binding, not a specific field); truncated per function.
        local_tainted_struct_bindings: Array[String]
        ; deep-R2 store-escape: locals bound with an escaping init lifetime
        ; (`^name`/`#`) or already-promoted, into which a put/add stores a value
        ; that must therefore also live on a non-local page.
        escaping_targets: Array[String]
        ; Page inference (address-taken rule): names a pre-scan found taken by
        ; address (`&NAME`) somewhere in the current function body. A frame-local
        ; Vector binding whose address is taken and referenced into an escaping
        ; structure (a PlannedCall/PlannedItem/PlannedFunction field) must live on
        ; the CALLER's page (rp), not the local page — exactly what an explicit
        ; `^rp` did. plan_binding# promotes such bindings; truncated per function.
        address_taken_locals: Array[String]
        ; Per-function set of names that head a member TARGET of a `set`
        ; (`set x.field: v`), collected by the same pre-scan. A `let` binding
        ; of such a name is planned as "slot": the emitter gives a record held
        ; as a VALUE a slot, so the store has an address (emit_binding#).
        field_written_locals: Array[String]
        ; the pending-type experiment: 0 not asked yet, 1 off, 2 on
        ; (SCALYC_PENDING, pending_on#)
        var pending_mode: int
        ; the operation census: slot 0 the switch, the rest its counters
        ; (operation_census#); from slot 23 the operation driver's state and
        ; counters (operation#)
        var operation_counts: Vector[size_t]
        ; Ref-flow provenance (2026-07-02f): per-function set of holder-style
        ; locals whose INTERIOR references (`H.get(i)` / `H.get_ptr(i)`)
        ; reach an argument or return position un-dereferenced — directly or
        ; via a `let r H.get(i)` / `set r: H.get(i)` alias (the two parallel
        ; alias arrays map alias name -> receiver). Such a reference lands in
        ; a structure that outlives the frame (record_function's
        ; PlannedFunction, MethodMatch^this), so plan_binding# promotes H's
        ; construction to the caller page exactly like the address-taken
        ; rule above (same gates). A DEREFERENCED read `*H.get(i)` copies
        ; the value out — benign, not recorded. Truncated per function.
        getref_arg_locals: Array[String]
        getref_alias_names: Array[String]
        getref_alias_receivers: Array[String]
        ; Option B (2026-07-02h checker net): per-function set of container
        ; holder locals whose page-owning construction REMAINED on the frame's
        ; local page after every promotion pass above (defaulted-Local life;
        ; not a parameter, not #/^ — those carry Call/Reference life — and not
        ; in promoted_binding_names). A `.get(i)`/`.get_ptr(i)` ref out of such
        ; a holder points into the local page; collapse_operand_sequence#'s
        ; method dispatch stamps those results Local-pointee (stamp_getref_
        ; local_pointee#) so the return/store/pass escape checks reject an
        ; escaping use instead of silently dangling. Truncated per function.
        ;
        ; The *_base fields index the CURRENT function's first entry: the
        ; name arrays are entry-truncated per function, NOT cleared, so a
        ; generic method lazily instantiated MID-function (lookup_method#'s
        ; s188 hook — e.g. VectorIterator[int].next during test_vector) sees
        ; the outer function's entries. A whole-array name scan then false-
        ; matches an outer holder (next's `vector` FIELD vs test_vector's
        ; `vector` local). The stamp and the no-rp gate consult only entries
        ; at index >= base; the pre-existing sets (promoted_binding_names,
        ; getref promotion) keep their whole-array scans — promotion is
        ; placement-only, and narrowing them would change emission.
        local_frame_holder_names: Array[String]
        var local_frame_holder_base: size_t
        var getref_arg_base: size_t
        ; Set true while planning the IMPLICIT-RETURN (tail) action of a
        ; function whose return value owns page guts (needs_implicit_return_
        ; page). plan_action# reads-and-clears it so only the OUTERMOST body
        ; action promotes; promote_tail_operand# then re-stamps the tail
        ; expression (and recurses into choose/if/match arms + variant
        ; payloads) to the caller page. Closes the implicit-return-of-a-
        ; construction/call gap that R2 (explicit `return` only) misses.
        var promote_tail_return: bool
        ; Set true while planning the IMPLICIT-RETURN (tail) action of ANY
        ; function with a return type (independent of promote_tail_return,
        ; which fires only for page-owning-value returns). plan_action# reads-
        ; and-clears it and runs check_tail_ref_escape# — the DIAGNOSTIC
        ; mirror of plan_return#'s reference-escape check. Closes hole 1
        ; (2026-07-02f): `return &local` was hard-errored but the same
        ; expression as a body tail (`{ ...; &result }`, unify) compiled
        ; clean and dangled.
        var check_tail_escape: bool
        ; true while the VALUE of a function's implicit return is planned --
        ; the if/choose/match arms and block tails inside it are return
        ; positions too (retarget_bare_variant#)
        var in_tail_value: bool
        instantiated_functions: Array[PlannedFunction]
        ; O(1) membership index over instantiated_functions' mangled names,
        ; maintained in lockstep at the single record_function add site.
        ; has_recorded_function was an O(n) linear scan called from 4 hot
        ; collapse_operand_sequence call-resolution sites -> O(n^2) over a
        ; -c scalyc compile (same pattern as lookup_concept / the emitter
        ; caches). lookup(mangled) >= 0 means present. Pure membership ->
        ; same bool -> identical Plan -> identical IR (fixpoint-safe).
        function_name_index: ConceptIndex
        instantiated_globals: Array[PlannedGlobal]

        ; s116: cycle detection for instantiate_generic#. Holds the
        ; mangled names of generics currently being planned but not yet
        ; recorded in instantiated_structures/_unions. Without this,
        ; cross-module nested-generic chains (e.g. scalyc.scaly compile)
        ; recurse plan_type# → instantiate_generic# → plan_structure# →
        ; plan_function# → plan_type# without bound and blow the stack
        ; in resolve_type_base#. Mirrors stage-0's FunctionsBeingPlanned
        ; set (Planner.cpp:11685).
        instantiating_structures: Array[String]

        ; s188: structure mangled names already lazily full-instantiated by
        ; lookup_method#'s call-site hook, so a re-entrant resolution of the
        ; same concept's method during its own body planning is a no-op.
        instantiating_methods: Array[String]

        ; Free-function registry. Entries are appended for every
        ; top-level Function encountered during the concept-registration
        ; pass. A single name can appear multiple times for overloads —
        ; lookup_functions returns all matches.
        model_functions: Array[KeyValuePair[String, Function]]

        ; Phase-1 lambdas: `let f \params: body` records the lambda model
        ; keyed by the binding name; calls to `f` monomorphize the body at
        ; the call site (see collapse_operand_sequence#). No runtime value
        ; is emitted for the binding.
        lambda_models: Array[KeyValuePair[String, Lambda]]

        ; O(1) name -> ordered registry-index chain over model_functions,
        ; maintained in lockstep at the single register_function add site.
        ; Replaces lookup_functions / is_function's O(n) linear scan (O(n^2)
        ; over a -c scalyc compile). Walks in insertion order -> identical
        ; overload-resolution input -> identical Plan -> identical IR.
        model_function_index: FunctionIndex

        ; Operator registry (same shape as model_functions; separate
        ; keyspace because an operator is identified by its symbol).
        model_operators: Array[KeyValuePair[String, Operator]]

        ; O(1) name -> ordered registry-index chain over model_operators,
        ; maintained in lockstep at the single register_operator add site.
        model_operator_index: FunctionIndex

        ; Set by resolve_operator_call# / resolve_prefix_operator_call# to the
        ; matched operator's intrinsic-ness (true iff its implementation is
        ; Intrinsic). Read by the operator-call construction in
        ; collapse_operand_sequence# ONLY when the resolve returned non-null,
        ; so a user-defined (Action-bodied) operator emits a real call to its
        ; mangled function instead of being routed to emit_intrinsic_*_op.
        ; Mirrors stage-0's OperatorMatch.IsIntrinsic (Planner.cpp:3118/4470).
        var resolved_op_is_intrinsic: bool

        ; Set by resolve_operator_call# to the matched operator's planned
        ; parameter types (excluding `this`) ONLY when the match succeeded via
        ; the char/float literal-fold pass — empty otherwise. Read by the binary
        ; operator-call construction in collapse_operand_sequence# so it can fold
        ; the bare String/f64 literal operands to the char/float parameter types
        ; (fold_string_args_to_char) and mangle the callee under those types. A
        ; non-fold match leaves it empty, keeping the construction byte-identical.
        var resolved_op_fold_params: Vector[PlannedType]

        ; Loaded-package cache — name → root Module. Populated lazily
        ; once a package is found and parsed.
        loaded_packages: Array[KeyValuePair[String, Module]]

        ; Intra-package module cache keyed by full file path.
        ; Pre-populated by the outer driver via
        ; register_loaded_module; load_intra_package_module#
        ; consults it on demand.
        loaded_module_paths: Array[KeyValuePair[String, Module]]

        ; Collected diagnostic messages. Planner paths that can't
        ; resolve a name / method / type push descriptive strings
        ; here instead of (or in addition to) falling back to a
        ; fresh type variable. The caller (cli.scaly, tests) can
        ; inspect errors.length > 0 to detect problems without
        ; plumbing Result[T, E] through every helper.
        errors: Array[String]

        ; HARD diagnostics. Unlike `errors` (advisory — speculative
        ; lookup misses land there thousands of times during a clean
        ; self-compile), an entry here means a construct WOULD be
        ; dropped from the emitted IR. cli.run prints these with
        ; file:line:col and fails the compile (s185, roadmap Step C).
        diagnostics: Array[PlannerDiagnostic]

        ; Sibling programs: Model.Programs belonging to the same
        ; compilation unit but loaded from separate source files.
        ; Mirrors Planner.cpp's SiblingPrograms — main.scaly may
        ; reference cli.scaly, which references compiler.scaly;
        ; once each has been parsed+modelled the result is stashed
        ; here so concepts/functions/operators from every sibling
        ; populate the same registries as the primary program.
        sibling_programs: Array[Program]

        ; Currently-active namespace context. Pushed when
        ; plan_namespace enters a `define cli { … }`-style block,
        ; popped on exit. Used by mangle_function to prefix
        ; namespace-scoped functions with the namespace name
        ; (Itanium `_ZN3cli4mainE…` shape) and by
        ; is_namespace_sibling_function to detect unqualified
        ; calls to peer functions of the enclosing namespace.
        var current_namespace: ref[Namespace]?
        var current_namespace_name: ref[String]?

        ; Currently-active STRUCTURE context. Pushed when plan_structure
        ; enters a `define Foo ( props ) { … }`-style concept and popped on
        ; exit. Unlike a namespace, a Structure's this-less (static) member
        ; functions are NOT registered in the free-function registry
        ; (register_concept_recursive's Structure arm records only nested
        ; concepts), so an unqualified sibling STATIC call inside another
        ; static of the same struct — `bit(m)` from `build_table()` in
        ; opensp's ModeInfo — has no `this` to drive the s101 dispatch and
        ; falls through to "function not found". This name lets the bare-call
        ; resolver retry against the enclosing struct's statics as a last
        ; resort (resolve_static_method_on#). Structure-shaped concepts with
        ; only functions and no properties are modeled as a Namespace, which
        ; already resolves their siblings; this covers the property-bearing
        ; ones. Null outside a Structure body.
        var current_structure_name: ref[String]?

        ; Last-segment names of every `use a.b.c` clause in the
        ; program's main module. Consulted by the dotted-call
        ; namespace override to decide whether a `prefix.suffix(...)`
        ; call should emit a real extern declaration even when
        ; planning is happening inside a loaded-package namespace
        ; body. Mirrors stage-0's Use-alias check at
        ; Planner.cpp:7752-7789. Populated once at plan_program#
        ; entry, stays empty until then.
        program_use_aliases: Array[String]

        ; Parallel to program_use_aliases: the second-to-last segment of
        ; each `use a.b.c` clause (the enclosing-namespace name for
        ; mangling), or empty when the clause has only one segment.
        ; Lets a bare call to a use-imported namespace member mangle as
        ; `_ZN<ns><fn>E…` (matching the member's definition) instead of
        ; the free `_Z<fn>…` (which would be undefined at link).
        program_use_namespaces: Array[String]

        ; Monotonic counter for synthesizing unique lambda names.
        var next_lambda_id: size_t

        ; Escape checker (RBMM blocker C), Vector 3 — interprocedural
        ; pass-to-storing-callee. Built by check_interprocedural_escapes#
        ; as a post-pass Plan-tree walk over instantiated_functions.
        ; escape_records is the summary (see EscapeRecord); escape_checking
        ; gates collect (false) vs check (true) mode; escape_fn_index names
        ; the function currently being walked (re-fetched via
        ; instantiated_functions.get to avoid storing its ref/String fields,
        ; which corrupt — s34); escape_found short-circuits once a violation
        ; has been reported.
        escape_records: Array[EscapeRecord]
        esc_index: ref[EscIndex]
        ; lookup_global#: name -> first position in instantiated_globals
        global_index: ref[NameIndex]
        ; is_concept_parameter_name#: every generic parameter name of every
        ; registered concept (the position is unused)
        concept_param_index: ref[NameIndex]
        member_index: ref[MemberIndex]
        var escape_checking: bool
        var escape_found: bool
        ; SCALYC_GATE_ALL: keep walking after a finding (a measurement of
        ; every site, not the one-at-a-time a fatal checker gives)
        var gate_all: bool
        var escape_fn_index: size_t

        ; Transitive Vector-3 (interprocedural pass-to-storing-callee closure).
        ; The direct pass records only functions that STORE a parameter frame-
        ; external; a function that merely FORWARDS its parameter on to a storing
        ; callee (forward(p) calls store(p)) gets no direct record, so
        ; forward(&local) is silently accepted (the one known soundness hole).
        ; escape_forwarding gates a fixpoint pass (between collect and check)
        ; that, at each call passing a bare reference-like/delegate PARAMETER
        ; into a callee's persisting slot, records that the current function
        ; persists that parameter too — repeated until no new fact appears.
        var escape_forwarding: bool

        ; True once the collect walk has seen any INDIRECT call. Indirect calls
        ; carry no summary record (no mangled callee), so the "records empty ->
        ; nothing to check" fast path would wrongly skip the indirect-call check;
        ; this flag keeps the fast path only for code with neither records nor
        ; indirect calls (the common, delegate-free case incl. the compiler).
        var escape_saw_indirect: bool

        ; the analysis state, one record per analysis (split off 2026-09-24:
        ; the Planner record had reached its page — 4 008 of 4 056 bytes)
        gate: ref[DeepGateState]
        residence: ref[ResidenceState]
        persistence: ref[PersistState]

        ret_res_names: Array[String]
        ret_res_starts: ref[Array[size_t]]
        ret_res_ends: ref[Array[size_t]]
        ret_res_class: Array[size_t]
        ret_res_arg: Array[size_t]
        ret_res_owner: Array[String]
        var census_trace: bool
        var bv_trace: bool
        ; per function of the post-pass: locals bound from a CALL and the
        ; by-value verdict of that call's result
        current_fn_bind_names: Array[String]
        ; local containers this body put a frame-local value INTO (the deep
        ; gate's element rule: a callee that keeps an ELEMENT of such a
        ; container keeps something local)
        ; ★★★ BEHIND A REFERENCE, like deep_scanned_owners: the Planner record
        ; is 4 040 bytes of a 4 096-byte page (2026-09-23), and one more
        ; embedded Array made it OVERSIZED — every Array field's Page.get then
        ; answered the oversized page and stage 1 died at exit 19.
        current_fn_local_filled: ref[Array[String]]
        current_fn_bind_local: Array[size_t]
        ; beside it, the REFERENCE verdict of the same bound call
        ; (ref_call_verdict#: -1 unknown, 0 not local, 1 local) — behind a
        ; reference for the Planner's page budget (see current_fn_local_filled)
        current_fn_bind_refv: ref[Array[int]]
        ; names bound from a call whose LIFE is Local: an object built on this
        ; frame (`var planner Planner.Planner(...)`), for reference_names_frame_local#
        current_fn_frame_objects: ref[Array[String]]

        var escape_census: bool
        ; set by cli BEFORE planning under --host-report: the persist summary
        ; is then computed for EVERY resolved callee, not only for one that
        ; met a local-life argument, so the census can ask it for any call.
        var host_report: bool
        census_names: Array[String]
        var census_box_start: size_t
        var census_escaped: bool
        ; The candidate's ADDRESS was persisted into a LOCAL keeper (`seen.add(&T^host(…))`,
        ; `let r &T^host(…)` + `list.add(r)`): the value never leaves the routine, but the
        ; de-sigiled spelling is a stack temporary whose slot every loop iteration reuses —
        ; `local-pinned`, never droppable (tscaly acid test 2026-09-23: three TS1117/TS1119
        ; units silent at rc 0, invisible to poison because no page is released).
        var census_pinned: bool
        var census_pinned_count: size_t
        census_files: Array[String]
        census_offsets: Array[size_t]
        census_texts: Array[String]
        var census_total: size_t
        var census_local: size_t

        ; CLOSURE Vector-3 support: names bound to a stack (Lifetime.Local)
        ; closure in the function CURRENTLY being walked by the escape
        ; post-pass. Rebuilt from the Plan tree (each function's PlannedBinding
        ; whose init is a Local Closure) at every escape_walk_function_body
        ; entry — NOT from the plan-time local_closure_names, which is
        ; truncated per function and stale by post-pass time. Lets the call
        ; check flag a stack closure passed to a storing callee.
        current_fn_closure_names: Array[String]

        ; Reshape provenance (escape post-pass): locals whose initializer derives
        ; from a reference-like/delegate PARAMETER of the function currently being
        ; walked, mapped to that parameter's index (parallel arrays, rebuilt per
        ; function like current_fn_closure_names). Lets the store/forward
        ; recorders see through an alias (`let q p; store(q)`) so a reshaped
        ; forward of a parameter is still attributed to it (closes the arg-reshape
        ; residual together with the member-access/deref/&-root provenance walk).
        current_fn_alias_names: Array[String]
        current_fn_alias_params: Array[size_t]

        ; FIELD-points-to map (Design-B 3e), rebuilt per function like the
        ; alias/closure sets. An entry (name, field, region) means "local
        ; `name`'s struct field at index `field` holds a reference of region
        ; rank `region`". Only LOCAL-region reference fields are recorded (the
        ; only flaggable ones) — a field left unrecorded resolves to Caller
        ; (safe) at region_of_arg_path#. Seeded from VISIBLE local constructions
        ; (`var pp Pair(&bx, ...)`): positional component i -> field i, region =
        ; region_of_operand(component). Call-result locals (`let s save_state()`)
        ; are deliberately NOT seeded: a function returning a struct whose
        ; reference field points to a frame-local is already rejected by the
        ; return-escape vectors, so such a local's fields are guaranteed non-
        ; local — leaving them unrecorded (Caller/safe) is sound. This is why
        ; an explicit return-field-provenance summary (3f) adds no flagging
        ; behavior over the return-escape guarantee; see region_of_arg_path#.
        current_fn_fpt_names: Array[String]
        current_fn_fpt_fields: Array[size_t]
        current_fn_fpt_regions: Array[size_t]
        ; locals bound from a VISIBLE positional construction (a stack
        ; record whose fields decide where its guts live), seeded beside
        ; the field map
        current_fn_ctor_locals: Array[String]

        ; Channel use-after-send move checker (milestone 1.4): per-function
        ; state, rebuilt per walked function like the alias/closure sets.
        ; move_moved_names holds binding names whose message region was
        ; transferred by a Channel.send (tombstoned to "" on re-binding);
        ; move_alias_names/roots map a bare reference alias (`let q m`) to
        ; its root name so a use of either flags. move_found short-circuits
        ; after the first reported violation.
        move_moved_names: Array[String]
        move_alias_names: Array[String]
        move_alias_roots: Array[String]
        var move_found: bool

        ; Transitive page-need set (interprocedural escape forwarding). Names
        ; of functions that need the caller's page ONLY because their body
        ; forwards rp to a sibling that needs one (the escape-machinery
        ; dispatchers: collect_flow_stmt, collect_returns_*, scan_top_level_
        ; returns — void, no own ^rp, but they thread rp down to a void ^rp
        ; storer like add_escaping_*). Computed once by build_transitive_page_
        ; set after registration; consulted by function_needs_caller_page.
        transitive_page_names: Array[String]
        ; R8 (2026-09-25): the routines that put a value built on their page into
        ; the state of their receiver or a parameter, directly or through a
        ; routine they hand that state to -- build_store_page_set#.
        store_page_names: ref[Array[String]]
        ; parallel to store_page_names: WHERE the routine stores -- bit 0 its
        ; receiver's state, bit k its k-th parameter -- and the accumulator
        ; the walk of one body fills
        store_page_masks: ref[Array[u64]]
        ; the NAMES of the routines in store_page_names (store_key's part before
        ; the `@`), for the Design-B flip: a call of such a routine keeps the
        ; caller page whatever is done with its result
        var store_name_index: ConceptIndex
        ; the MANGLED names of planned routines with a nonzero R8 mask, the
        ; mask, and whether argument 0 is the receiver -- what R8's net asks
        ; at a call
        store_mangled_recv: ref[Array[String]]
        store_mangled_masks: ref[Array[u64]]
        store_mangled_this: ref[Array[bool]]
        var store_acc: u64
        ; the bindings and `set`s the store walk has passed in the routine it
        ; walks, for stored_value_builds# to follow a stored local
        store_scope: ref[Array[Statement]]
        ; ★A SEPARATE set for the return-position rule, and separate on purpose:
        ; impl_mentions_set propagates out of transitive_page_names by NAME
        ; MENTION, so feeding this rule's hits back into that set cascades — one
        ; newly promoted name pulls in every function that merely mentions it.
        ; Measured on packages/opensp: 1216 of 5185 functions flipped, among them
        ; void ones like InputSource.start_token that cannot possibly return a
        ; page-hosted value. function_needs_caller_page reads both sets; the
        ; mention rule reads only the transitive one.
        ; ★And keyed by function IDENTITY, not by name: a NAME identifies a
        ; function only when it is unique, and the whole point of the receiver-
        ; type resolution below is that same-named siblings decide differently
        ; (`EntityCatalog.lookup` returns a String, `IdTable.lookup` hands out a
        ; borrowed pointer). The key is name + span, i.e. the three arrays are
        ; parallel; a promotion therefore never leaks onto a sibling, and every
        ; sibling gets judged on its own in the fixpoint.
        return_page_names: Array[String]
        return_page_starts: Array[size_t]
        return_page_ends: Array[size_t]
        ; build_transitive_page_set's two scratch sets for the NAME-KEYED
        ; FALLBACK path of the return-position rule (a receiver whose type this
        ; pre-pass cannot resolve — a local bound to a call, a deref hop). Fields
        ; rather than locals so the walkers can take the Planner instead of
        ; threading four sets through ten recursive signatures. Empty outside the
        ; pre-pass.
        prepass_direct_names: Array[String]
        prepass_veto_names: Array[String]
        var transitive_page_built: bool


        ; ===== use-visibility infrastructure (stage 1: recording only) =====
        ; Rust-style strict `use` visibility for concepts. Stage 1 only
        ; RECORDS — no lookup consults these yet, so behavior/emission is
        ; unchanged. The function-alias machinery (program_use_aliases/
        ; _namespaces) stays untouched; this is the parallel CONCEPT
        ; structure.
        ; concept_origin_files/paths run in LOCKSTEP with `concepts` (same
        ; index): the defining module's file and dotted container path
        ; (module chain + enclosing namespaces, e.g. "scaly.containers"
        ; for Vector). registering_file/path are the save/switch/restore
        ; context maintained by register_module_concepts /
        ; register_concept_recursive during registration walks (empty =
        ; unknown origin).
        ; use_vis_files/names/paths are flat per-use-clause triples: the
        ; recording module's file, the clause's last path segment ("*"
        ; marks a glob — `use ns.*` parses with a trailing "*" segment),
        ; and the dotted prefix (segments before the last — the namespace
        ; the name is imported from). use_vis_recorded_files dedups
        ; modules walked more than once (register_packages + plan_module).
        ; Declared LAST (field-index stability, persist_summary lesson).
        concept_origin_files: Array[String]
        concept_origin_paths: Array[String]
        var registering_file: String
        var registering_path: String
        use_vis_files: Array[String]
        use_vis_names: Array[String]
        use_vis_paths: Array[String]
        use_vis_recorded_files: Array[String]

        ; Stage 2 (advisory measurement, cli --use-vis): every
        ; lookup_concept hit is classified against the would-be strict
        ; visibility rules (local > explicit use > glob > prelude;
        ; unknown origin = lenient) and counted — NO behavior change.
        ; Misses dedup by (concept index, current file);
        ; use_vis_miss_lazy notes whether the first sighting happened
        ; during lazy instantiation (instantiating_structures/methods
        ; non-empty) — those point at the visibility-context switch
        ; (nested-planning trap), not at a missing use line.
        var use_vis_measure: bool
        var use_vis_hits: size_t
        var use_vis_misses: size_t
        use_vis_miss_idx: Array[size_t]
        use_vis_miss_files: Array[String]
        use_vis_miss_lazy: Array[size_t]

        ; Anchored container path per recorded module file, in LOCKSTEP
        ; with use_vis_recorded_files. A registration walk starting
        ; mid-tree (plan_module(sub) re-walks with an empty inherited
        ; prefix) looks its file up here so re-registered concepts keep
        ; root-anchored origins.
        use_vis_recorded_paths: Array[String]

        ; Stage 4 (enforcement, default ON — cli --no-use-vis disables):
        ; an INVISIBLE concept behaves as NOT FOUND (lookup_concept
        ; returns null) so the existing not-found machinery fires; the
        ; exhaustion diagnostic appends an "add: use ..." hint via
        ; use_vis_hint/lookup_concept_any. LENIENT during lazy
        ; instantiation (nested-planning trap: a generic's body planned
        ; mid-module must see its OWN module's names; the context switch
        ; is unimplemented — stdlib machinery is prelude-visible anyway).
        ; The verdict is memoized per (concept index, current file) in
        ; the two lockstep-with-`concepts` arrays below (verdict_file
        ; empty = uncomputed; ok 1 = visible).
        var use_vis_enforce: bool
        use_vis_verdict_file: Array[String]
        use_vis_verdict_ok: Array[size_t]

        ; --jit only: plan the BODIES of non-prelude loaded packages (so a
        ; program that imports e.g. `package opensp 0.1.0` and calls into it
        ; actually JIT-compiles and RUNS those bodies, instead of leaving them
        ; as undefined externals that the ORC JIT resolves to a 0-returning
        ; stub — the false-pass that hid every opensp unit test). OFF for AOT /
        ; the seed build, where dependency bodies come from a linked archive and
        ; planning them here would emit ret-stubs that collide at link time
        ; (see plan_program's note). Guarded so non-JIT emission is byte-identical.
        jit_dep_bodies: bool

        ; Membership set of names registered by ≥2 concepts from DIFFERENT
        ; origin files — the glob-type-disambiguation fast path. Maintained
        ; incrementally in register_concept (a cross-origin duplicate is the
        ; only case an explicit `use ns.Name` can change resolution). O(1)
        ; hashed (same ConceptIndex as concept_index) so lookup_concept's hot
        ; path pays only one hash probe per call; the explicit-use scan runs
        ; ONLY for the few genuinely cross-origin-duplicated names. The
        ; compiler shares several such names (Model/Plan/Syntax Call, Type,
        ; Lifetime, Span, …) but explicitly uses none of them.
        ambiguous_index: ConceptIndex

        ; Cache of the current file's EXPLICIT (non-glob) use names. `file` is
        ; set once (init) and never changes, so this stays valid; rebuilt only
        ; when use_vis_files grows. Turns the per-call explicit-use test (for
        ; the cross-origin-duplicated names that pass ambiguous_index) from an
        ; O(all-use-clauses) linear scan into an O(this-file's-explicit-uses)
        ; one. Rebuilt INLINE (a nested procedure call did not persist the
        ; mutation; inline mutation from a function does — cf. use_vis_check's
        ; verdict memo).
        file_explicit_names: Array[String]

        ; TRUE iff some name is BOTH cross-origin-ambiguous AND explicitly
        ; used in the main file — the only situation where glob-type-
        ; disambiguation changes a result. Set once during registration (both
        ; add-sites), so lookup_concept's hot path pays just a single bool read
        ; and skips the disambiguation entirely otherwise. FALSE for the
        ; compiler's own source (its cross-origin names — Model/Plan/Syntax
        ; Call/Type/… — are never explicitly used), so the self-compile hot
        ; path is unchanged.
        var disambiguation_active: bool

        ; s200: scope-isolation floor for nested lazy instantiation.
        ; lookup_local_binding's reverse scan stops HERE instead of 0.
        ; instantiate_generic sets it to scope_bindings.length while
        ; planning an instantiation's bodies (save/restore discipline),
        ; so a generic method's bare member names can NEVER resolve to
        ; the ENCLOSING function's same-named locals — the s02i nested-
        ; planning scope leak. Concretely: planning VectorIterator[BL]'s
        ; next() nested under a user main that has a local named
        ; `vector` resolved the body's bare `vector` (a PROPERTY) to
        ; main's local → unplannable in the method → statements silently
        ; dropped → the recorded instantiation had a degenerate next()
        ; (rehash lost all entries; HashMapBuilder.add after init).
        ; Lambdas are unaffected (planned in-function, not through
        ; instantiate_generic — capture discovery still sees the
        ; enclosing scope). Declared LAST (field-index stability).
        var scope_isolation_base: size_t

        ; Byte offset of each recorded use clause, in lockstep with
        ; use_vis_files/names/paths — the anchor check_use_paths# reports at.
        ; module_paths is every dotted MODULE path the registration walk saw:
        ; a `use` may name a module rather than a concept (`use
        ; scaly.memory.runtime`, a module of extern functions), and no
        ; concept origin records such a path.
        ; Declared LAST (field-index stability).
        use_vis_starts: Array[size_t]
        module_paths: Array[String]
        ; Container path of each registered Function, in lockstep with
        ; model_functions — a `use` may name a FUNCTION (`use
        ; scaly.memory.runtime.system`), and only its origin can tell an
        ; honest such clause from one naming a path nothing lives at.
        function_origin_paths: Array[String]
        ; parallel to the registry too: the NAMESPACE a function is a member
        ; of ("" for a free function) -- lookup_functions_preferring#
        function_namespaces: ref[Array[String]]
        var registering_namespace: String
        ; parallel to the registry as well: the source FILE a function was
        ; registered from ("" outside a module walk) -- function_origin_file#
        function_origin_files: ref[Array[String]]
        ; the start offsets of variant constructions made from a BARE name
        ; (`Hit(5)`, `Blue`) -- retarget_bare_variant# may move them onto the
        ; union a declared target type names
        bare_variant_starts: ref[Array[size_t]]

        ; Stack guard (check_nesting_stack#). stack_base is the SHALLOWEST
        ; plan_operand frame address seen so far, i.e. the top of the recursive
        ; descent — kept as a running maximum instead of being captured at a
        ; designated entry point, so it needs no knowledge of who starts the
        ; walk and self-recalibrates if planning ever runs on another stack
        ; (a promoted `for` worker). stack_budget is the byte budget derived
        ; ONCE from scaly_stack_limit; 0 in both means "not yet established".
        ; Declared LAST (field-index stability).
        var stack_base: size_t
        var stack_budget: size_t
    )
    {
        init(filename: String) linked

        ; ================================================================
        ; Scope management (mirrors Planner::pushScope etc. in C++).
        ; ================================================================

        procedure push_scope(mutable this: Planner) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true linked

        procedure pop_scope(mutable this: Planner) @deep true linked

        ; mark the innermost binding of `name` as a `when` arm's COPY
        procedure mark_when_copy(mutable this: Planner, name: String) @deep true @deep_this_2 true @deep_whole_2 true linked

        ; `set a.x: v` where `a` is a `when` binding: the binding is a COPY of the
        ; arm (and the choose itself copies its subject), so a member store into
        ; it can never reach the union — and the emitter GEP'd through the LOADED
        ; payload VALUE, a store to wherever its first eight bytes pointed: exit
        ; 139 when they were not an address, a silent wrong write when they were.
        ; The idiom is copy out, change, write the arm back (89 sites in tscaly).
        procedure report_when_binding_store(mutable this: Planner, target: Vector[Operand]) @deep true linked

        procedure define_local(
            mutable this: Planner,
            name: String,
            t: PlannedType,
            is_mutable: bool,
            is_on_page: bool
        ) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_this_6 true @deep_whole_6 true linked

        ; Define a function/init/operator parameter (or `this`/page receiver).
        ; Like define_local but marks the binding frame-EXTERNAL for the escape
        ; checker: a struct/pointer parameter is passed by pointer, so writing
        ; `param.field` stores into the CALLER's object (which outlives this
        ; frame's local page). Mirrors Planner.cpp::defineParameter.
        procedure define_parameter(mutable this: Planner, name: String, t: PlannedType) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_this_6 true @deep_whole_6 true linked

        ; lookup_local_binding: innermost-first reverse scan. Returns
        ; null if no binding with this name is live in any current scope.
        ; The scan floor is scope_isolation_base (0 normally): while an
        ; instantiate_generic body plan is active, bindings BELOW the
        ; floor belong to the enclosing function and must be invisible
        ; (s200 — see the field comment).
        function lookup_local_binding(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[LocalBinding]? linked

        function lookup_local(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[PlannedType]? linked

        ; ================================================================
        ; Generic type-parameter substitutions.
        ; ================================================================

        procedure define_type_substitution(mutable this: Planner, name: String, t: PlannedType) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_this_6 true @deep_whole_6 true linked

        procedure clear_type_substitutions(mutable this: Planner) @deep true linked

        function lookup_type_substitution(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[PlannedType]? linked

        ; enter_concept_generic_scope / leave_concept_generic_scope: reason
        ; about the DECLARATIONS of the generic concept `name` under exactly
        ; its own parameter bindings, taken from the generic ARGUMENTS of the
        ; type being constructed — and under nothing else.
        ;
        ; An initializer's parameter types are written in the concept's own
        ; scope: `Vector[T].init(array: Array[T])` means Vector's T, and no
        ; other T can be meant. Planning them against whatever bindings happen
        ; to sit on the substitution stack is how a construction silently
        ; miscompiled: matching `Vector[KeyValuePair[K, Nameable]]`'s
        ; initializers in the scalyls root, a leftover `T = Function` from an
        ; unrelated instantiation was in scope, so every arity-1 init was
        ; compared against the parameter type `Array[Function]`, none matched,
        ; and the construction fell through to the positional-tuple path —
        ; which bit-copied the Array HEADER into the Vector, leaving `data`
        ; holding the array's inner Vector pointer so every slot read the
        ; header instead of the elements. The scalyc root had no stale T in
        ; scope and compiled the same source correctly: that is what made the
        ; defect a function of the compilation ROOT rather than of the code.
        ;
        ; REPLACING the stack matters, twice over — both alternatives were
        ; measured and both fail. Merely HIDING the foreign binding is not
        ; enough: the argument types are already substituted and concrete, so
        ; an unsubstituted `Array[T]` on the parameter side stops matching the
        ; constructions that work today (`ArrayIterator`, `Vector` in the
        ; stdlib went unmatched at once). And merely LAYERING the concept's
        ; own bindings on top diverges, because the substitution is then
        ; applied to its own result: a generic argument may mention the same
        ; parameter name it binds (`HashSetBuilder[T]` constructing
        ; `VectorIterator[List[Slot[T]]]`), and re-expanding that on
        ; every pass drove the planner into its own stack guard (exit 17 at
        ; 52 MB). One simultaneous substitution, from an empty stack, is the
        ; only shape that is both correct and terminating.
        ;
        ; Not entered in three cases, each of which keeps today's behaviour
        ; exactly (answer null; leave# is then a no-op):
        ;
        ;   - a non-generic concept — there is nothing to bind;
        ;   - fewer arguments than parameters (the template pass) — there is
        ;     nothing to bind the rest TO, and the ambient fresh type
        ;     variables are exactly what those uses need;
        ;   - an argument that MENTIONS one of the parameter names it would
        ;     bind. That is the occurs-check, and it is what keeps this
        ;     terminating. `HashSetBuilder[T]` constructs
        ;     `VectorIterator[List[Slot[T]]]`, binding the callee's `T`
        ;     to a type containing a `T` of its own; substituting under that
        ;     binding grows the type on every pass. Both ways of getting it
        ;     wrong were measured: layering the binding on the ambient stack
        ;     recursed into the planner's stack guard (exit 17 at 52 MB), and
        ;     replacing the stack without this check ran the self-compile past
        ;     10 minutes and 3.1 GB instead of 35 seconds. A construction that
        ;     feeds its own parameter name back in keeps the old path.
        ; ★The `ref[…]?` return PAYS A CALLER PAGE here and that is deliberate:
        ; an NPO return asks `body_may_allocate`, and this body constructs the
        ; snapshot Array. The construction is pinned on `^this`, so the page is
        ; not actually needed -- but `body_may_allocate` is conservative BY
        ; DESIGN and tightening it to spare this case would take pages away
        ; elsewhere, the one direction that is a memory fault. The honest
        ; spelling costs one argument; `concept_origin_file` is the sibling
        ; whose body allocates nothing and converts free.
        procedure enter_concept_generic_scope(
            mutable this: Planner,
            name: String,
            struct_type: PlannedType
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_param_0 true @deep true @deep_this_4 true returns ref[Array[KeyValuePair[String, PlannedType]]]? linked

        procedure leave_concept_generic_scope(
            mutable this: Planner,
            snapshot: ref[Array[KeyValuePair[String, PlannedType]]]?
        ) @deep true @deep_this_2 true linked

        ; ================================================================
        ; Fresh type variables for HM inference.
        ; ================================================================

        procedure fresh_type_var_id(mutable this: Planner) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns size_t linked

        ; ================================================================
        ; Concept registry.
        ; ================================================================

        ; ====================================================================
        ; Two silent collisions inside ONE concept, each measured in a port
        ; before it was diagnosed (CLAUDE.md, "Calls & operands"):
        ;
        ;   (a) a FIELD DECLARED TWICE. `define Box (v: int  w: int  v: int)`
        ;       lays out three slots at rc 0; the second `v` is real layout
        ;       nobody can reach and every read resolves to the first. Nothing
        ;       looks wrong anywhere -- the two declarations may be four
        ;       hundred lines apart and both readers agree, because they read
        ;       the same slot. It cost `packages/tscaly` a shared memo: two
        ;       resolvers of one name answering out of one cell.
        ;
        ;   (b) a METHOD NAMED LIKE A FIELD. The field shadows it at every call
        ;       site: the argument is dropped and the call answers the FIELD's
        ;       value, rc 0, no diagnostic. For a `bool` field that reads as a
        ;       broken predicate -- a `skip_trivia` field beside a
        ;       `skip_trivia` method meant every diagnostic that had to move
        ;       past leading trivia was reported at offset 0.
        ;
        ; ★Reported at the SECOND declaration, which is the one to delete or
        ; rename, and with the concept named: the shape gives a reader nothing
        ; to search for otherwise.
        ;
        ; ★It runs at REGISTRATION and only on a concept's FIRST one, because a
        ; module tree is walked more than once (register_packages, then
        ; plan_module, then its submodules again) -- plan_structure would have
        ; been the obvious place and reports once per generic INSTANTIATION.
        ; A file whose origin is unknown is left alone, as every other
        ; registration-time judgement here is.
        ; ====================================================================
        procedure check_concept_member_names(mutable this: Planner, c: Concept, file: String) @deep true linked

        ; ====================================================================
        ; (c) A `this` METHOD AND A STATIC of one concept with the same name
        ; and the same parameter list mangle to ONE Itanium symbol, and the
        ; emitter keeps ONE body (CLAUDE.md; CLAUDE-history.md 2026-09-08).
        ; Neither the receiver nor the implicit caller page is in the name, so
        ; `f(this, x: T)` and `f(x: T)` are `_ZN3Box4pickER3Box` twice over --
        ; the linker has nothing to object to, because only one definition
        ; exists, and the other call form then passes its first argument into
        ; the wrong slot. Twenty lines compile with no diagnostic and exit 139.
        ;
        ; ★It stays LATENT while either call form is unreachable: in
        ; packages/tscaly the static's one caller sat behind a stop, and 28
        ; stage-2 units began crashing the moment that arm was ported.
        ;
        ; Reported at the STATIC, which is the form to keep where the function
        ; reads only its arguments -- so the fix is usually to drop the method.
        ; ====================================================================
        procedure check_method_static_collisions(mutable this: Planner, s: Structure, owner: String, file: String) @deep true linked

        ; The method's parameters WITHOUT its leading `this`, against the
        ; static's: that pair is exactly what the mangled name encodes.
        function methods_share_parameter_types(m: Function, st: Function) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; Structural equality of two WRITTEN types: the name segments and the
        ; generic arguments. A lifetime says nothing about a mangled name.
        function model_types_equal(a: ref[Type]?, b: ref[Type]?) @ret_param_1 true @deep true returns bool linked

        function function_declares_this(f: Function) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        function structure_has_property_named(this: Planner, s: Structure, name: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; The message and the file outlive this frame in this.diagnostics, so
        ; both live on this's region (check_use_paths' lesson).
        procedure report_member_collision(mutable this: Planner, loc: Span, file: String, kind: String, name: String, owner: String, tail: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true linked

        procedure register_concept(mutable this: Planner, c: Concept) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_this_2 true @deep_whole_2 true linked

        procedure lookup_concept(mutable this: Planner, name: String) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Concept]? linked

        ; lookup_concept_any: raw registry lookup WITHOUT the strict
        ; visibility gate — for diagnostics ("exists but not visible")
        ; and tooling.
        function lookup_concept_any(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Concept]? linked

        ; lookup_concept_for_layout: concept lookup for PHYSICAL-LAYOUT
        ; computation (compute_payload_size#, ensure_member_layout#,
        ; ensure_union_layout#). Visibility is a USER name-resolution concern,
        ; NOT a layout one: a cross-package by-value field/variant type the
        ; consumer did not `use` is invisible to lookup_concept (returns null),
        ; so it would size to the 8-byte get_primitive_alignment default and
        ; UNDER-SIZE the enclosing struct/union — the defining package lays it
        ; out at its real size, so json.get's sret store overruns the caller's
        ; slot and smashes the adjacent stack (the JsonValue.Array→JsonArray
        ; by-value case). Gated-first so glob-disambiguation still steers the
        ; visible case; the raw-registry fallback fires ONLY where
        ; lookup_concept already returns null, so the compiler's own emission
        ; is byte-identical (fixpoint-safe) and the fix is strictly additive
        ; (null-default-8 → real-size). Genuinely-unknown names stay null.
        procedure lookup_concept_for_layout(mutable this: Planner, name: String) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Concept]? linked

        ; s177: like lookup_concept but only matches a concept whose
        ; definition is a Namespace. `define Planner { define Planner(...) }`
        ; registers both under one name, and lookup_concept's backward
        ; scan (s95) returns the inner Structure. Qualified calls
        ; (`Planner.make_void_type()` from Emitter) need the OUTER
        ; Namespace to mangle with the namespace parent — without it the
        ; whole binding statement silently drops. Mirrors stage-0's
        ; qualified-call path (Planner.cpp:8604), whose concept cache
        ; holds the Namespace for the colliding name.
        function lookup_namespace_concept(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Concept]? linked

        procedure has_concept(mutable this: Planner, name: String) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; GAP B delegate (function-pointer type) helpers. A delegate concept
        ; `define Cb delegate(A, B) returns R` names a function type; a lambda
        ; or function whose signature matches is assignable to a Cb-typed
        ; binding/parameter, and a Cb-typed value is callable indirectly.
        procedure is_delegate_type(mutable this: Planner, name: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; The delegate's parameter types as planned types (drives lambda
        ; monomorphization against the declared signature).
        procedure delegate_param_types(mutable this: Planner, mutable name: String) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true @deep true returns Vector[PlannedType] io linked

        procedure delegate_return_type(mutable this: Planner, mutable name: String) @page true @direct true @deep true returns PlannedType io linked

        ; Instantiation-cache recording helpers. Called as each planned
        ; aggregate is produced so the final Plan lists everything the
        ; emitter needs to lower.
        ; ★★★TWO RECORDS THAT MANGLE TO ONE STRUCT AND DISAGREE ABOUT THEIR
        ; LAYOUT (2026-09-20). A top-level define mangles without its module
        ; and without its package -- mangle_structure# reads the name and the
        ; generic arguments, nothing else -- so two same-named records that
        ; both reach one compilation collapse into ONE LLVM struct and every
        ; field access in the loser reads the other's layout. In
        ; packages/tscaly a bool on a ptr slot made `store i1 true` write into
        ; a pointer's low byte and the reader's `return tp.is_this_type` was
        ; DROPPED, false for the whole tree for four slices; planner and linker
        ; said nothing, and a second pair with the same LLVM shape was harmless
        ; by luck.
        ;
        ; ★★★Asked HERE, where a LAYOUT is recorded, and not at registration --
        ; which was tried first and is WRONG: two same-named records in two
        ; modules are a language feature (`use ns.Name` steers type resolution,
        ; gates glob_ambig_disambig / glob_disambig_field, whose two `Widget`s
        ; have different field lists ON PURPOSE). What is legal is declaring
        ; them; what cannot work is LAYING OUT both. So the condition is the
        ; one that actually breaks: the same mangled name with a different
        ; field list.
        procedure record_structure(mutable this: Planner, s: PlannedStructure) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_this_2 true @deep_whole_2 true linked

        procedure check_layout_collision(mutable this: Planner, s: PlannedStructure) @deep true @deep_this_2 true linked

        ; Does any recorded `use` clause name this concept explicitly?
        function name_is_explicitly_used(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        function layout_field_count(a: PlannedStructure) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns size_t linked

        ; The name of the first field the two disagree about -- the detail a
        ; reader needs to tell "two records" from "one record planned twice".
        function first_layout_difference(a: PlannedStructure, b: PlannedStructure) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true @deep true returns String linked

        ; Two layouts differ when their field lists do: the count, a name or a
        ; field's mangled type. The OFFSETS are derived from those, so they add
        ; nothing.
        function planned_layouts_differ(a: PlannedStructure, b: PlannedStructure) @ret_param_1 true @deep true returns bool linked

        procedure record_union(mutable this: Planner, u: scalyc.compiler.Plan.PlannedUnion) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_this_2 true @deep_whole_2 true linked

        ; Lookups against the instantiation caches, keyed by mangled
        ; name. Used by instantiate_generic to avoid re-planning an
        ; already-instantiated concept.
        function has_instantiated_structure(this: Planner, mangled: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        function has_instantiated_union(this: Planner, mangled: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; Stage 7.2: instantiation-cache lookups returning the entry itself.
        ; Mangled-first two-pass (the Emitter's map_type order): generic
        ; instantiations share the bare name, the mangled name is the
        ; specialised key; the bare-name pass serves non-generic concepts
        ; whose PlannedType arrived without a mangled name.
        function lookup_instantiated_structure(this: Planner, mangled: String, name: String) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[PlannedStructure]? linked

        function lookup_instantiated_union(this: Planner, mangled: String, name: String) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[scalyc.compiler.Plan.PlannedUnion]? linked

        procedure record_function(mutable this: Planner, f: PlannedFunction) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_this_2 true @deep_whole_2 true linked

        ; Has a function with this mangled name already been recorded in
        ; instantiated_functions? Used by the dotted-call namespace
        ; override to avoid re-declaring an extern stub for a function
        ; that was already planned (or already extern-declared).
        function has_recorded_function(this: Planner, mangled: String) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns bool linked

        procedure record_global(mutable this: Planner, g: PlannedGlobal) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_this_2 true @deep_whole_2 true linked

        ; Enter `name` at `pos` unless it is already there (first entry wins);
        ; past half load the table doubles and every key is entered again.
        procedure name_index_put(mutable this: Planner, mutable ix: ref[NameIndex], name: String, pos: size_t) @deep true @deep_into_1_2 true linked

        ; lookup_global: locate a previously planned PlannedGlobal by name.
        ; Used by plan_type_operand#'s constant-folding path so a bare
        ; identifier like F_OK / SEEK_END resolves to its define value
        ; instead of falling through to PlannedExpression.Type (which
        ; emit_call drops as an arg, silently dropping the whole
        ; statement). Mirrors Planner.cpp:6950-6995.
        ; page_size_constant: the stdlib's PAGE_SIZE as this compilation sees it
        ; (the prelude's `define PAGE_SIZE`), or 0 when it is not in reach
        ; (--no-prelude). The Emitter checks against it that a record which
        ; derives its page from its own address fits on one (check_receiver_
        ; fits_page#).
        function page_size_constant(this: Planner) @deep true returns size_t linked

        ; plan_foreign_constant: a module-level constant of ANOTHER package
        ; than the root's is planned where it is first named. Constants are
        ; planned in module declaration order during the module walk, and the
        ; walk never enters a dependency package, so a generic body of the http
        ; package instantiated from https (serve_connection[TlsStream]) could
        ; not read http's own IN_SIZE, and a program none of its dependency's
        ; constants. A constant is emitted `internal` into every module that
        ; reads it, as its own package does. Within the root's package the
        ; module order stays the rule (use_vis_hint# says so).
        procedure plan_foreign_constant(mutable this: Planner, name: String) @page true @stores true @deep true io linked

        function lookup_global(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[PlannedGlobal]? linked

        ; ================================================================
        ; On-demand module loading.
        ;
        ; The Scaly Planner cannot yet drive the Scaly Parser +
        ; Modeler from its own body (would require threading a
        ; Parser instance or reifying a free-function parse entry
        ; point). Instead we take the pre-parsed-cache approach:
        ; an outer driver (cli.scaly, test harness) parses and
        ; models the required packages / sibling modules, then
        ; hands the resulting Module roots to the Planner via
        ; register_loaded_package / register_loaded_module. The
        ; load_* helpers are pure cache lookups.
        ;
        ; build_package_file_path# stays for callers that need to
        ; locate a candidate file before parsing it.
        ; ================================================================

        ; add_sibling_program: register another Program (typically
        ; built from a peer source file) whose top-level concepts,
        ; functions and operators should be visible during planning
        ; of the primary program. The sibling is stashed here and
        ; later surfaced by register_sibling_programs right before
        ; the main-module registration pass.
        procedure add_sibling_program(mutable this: Planner, prog: Program) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_this_2 true @deep_whole_2 true linked

        ; record_program_uses: walk the program's main module Use
        ; clauses and store each clause's last path segment in
        ; program_use_aliases. Called once from plan_program# before
        ; plan_module# runs. The resulting alias list lets the
        ; dotted-call extern-decl gate fire for `prefix.suffix(...)`
        ; calls even when planning a loaded-package namespace body,
        ; provided the prefix names a Use-imported alias of the main
        ; program. Mirrors stage-0's Use-alias filter at
        ; Planner.cpp:7752-7789.
        procedure record_program_uses(mutable this: Planner, prog: Program) @deep true linked

        ; lookup_use_alias_namespace: scan program_use_aliases in parallel
        ; with program_use_namespaces; return the namespace segment of the
        ; use clause whose last segment equals `name`, or empty if none.
        function lookup_use_alias_namespace(this: Planner, name: String) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true @deep true returns String linked

        ; use_alias_member_namespace: the namespace a use-imported `name` is a
        ; MEMBER of -- the alias's second-to-last segment, when a function of
        ; that name is registered in a namespace of that name -- else "". The
        ; segment can name a MODULE (`use tls.Crypto.sha256`), whose functions
        ; are free and mangle `_Z<fn>`; taken as a namespace, the call went to
        ; `_ZN6Crypto…`, which nothing defines (a link error, 2026-09-29).
        function use_alias_member_namespace(this: Planner, name: String) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true @deep true returns String linked

        ; is_program_use_alias: linear scan of program_use_aliases.
        function is_program_use_alias(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; ===== use-visibility recording (stage 1, advisory) =====
        ; Parallel CONCEPT-visibility structure — the function-alias
        ; machinery above (record_program_uses etc.) stays untouched.

        ; use_vis_file_recorded: has this module file's use clauses
        ; already been recorded? Modules are walked more than once
        ; (cli.register_packages + plan_module both call
        ; register_module_concepts on the same trees).
        function use_vis_file_recorded(this: Planner, f: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; use_vis_lookup_file_path: the anchored container path recorded
        ; at this file's first registration walk ("" when unknown).
        function use_vis_lookup_file_path(this: Planner, f: String) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true @deep true returns String linked

        ; dotted_use_prefix: join a use clause's path segments EXCEPT the
        ; last with "." — the namespace prefix the name (or glob) is
        ; imported from. `use scaly.containers.String` -> "scaly.containers";
        ; single-segment clauses -> "".
        procedure dotted_use_prefix(mutable this: Planner, path: Vector[String]) @page true @direct true @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns String linked

        ; extend_dotted_path: append `name` to a dotted container path.
        ; Used to maintain registering_path while descending modules and
        ; namespace-shaped concepts during registration. A name equal to
        ; the path's LAST segment is collapsed — the module-named-after-
        ; namespace idiom (module Modeler containing `define Modeler`,
        ; `define scalyc` in the program module scalyc) is addressed
        ; once, matching how use paths spell it.
        procedure extend_dotted_path(mutable this: Planner, prefix: String, name: String) @page true @direct true @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns String linked

        ; record_module_uses: record one module's use clauses into the
        ; use-visibility triples (file, last segment, dotted prefix).
        ; A trailing "*" segment is a glob import of the prefix namespace
        ; and is stored with name "*". Dedup'd per module file; NOT
        ; recursive — register_module_concepts' own recursion reaches
        ; every sub-module (including namespace-hosted ones).
        procedure record_module_uses(mutable this: Planner, m: Module) @deep true linked

        ; check_use_paths: every recorded `use` clause must name something that
        ; actually exists at that path.
        ;
        ; A `use` with a bogus MODULE PATH used to compile in total silence:
        ; `use opensp.Attribute.AttributeList` (AttributeList lives in module
        ; AttributeList, not Attribute) said nothing, and the cross-package
        ; method call it was supposed to make visible mis-emitted just as
        ; silently — constant false. The clause is a claim about where a name
        ; lives; if no registered concept agrees, the claim is wrong.
        ;
        ; The predicate is use_vis_target_match#, the SAME one lookup_concept
        ; uses to decide whether an explicit use steers a name — so anything
        ; this rejects could never have steered anything either.
        ; `use ns.*` is satisfied by any concept whose container IS ns.
        ; Functions and operators are not use-gated, so a path that only ever
        ; held functions is not something a `use` can name — those clauses
        ; were no-ops before and are reported now.
        ; use_path_names_origin: does `origin` (a concept's or function's
        ; ABSOLUTE container path) answer to the path a use clause spells?
        ; Two degrees of freedom, both real:
        ;   - the clause may name the container or the container's PARENT (the
        ;     `scaly.memory.Page` idiom, where the module is named after the
        ;     concept it holds) — hence prefix vs full;
        ;   - the clause is RELATIVE to where it is written: `use util.compute`
        ;     inside a namespace names an origin anchored as
        ;     "<module>.util". So a trailing segment match counts.
        ; It stays strict where it matters: `scaly.memory` is NOT a tail of
        ; `scaly.memory.root_pages`, so a concept named at the wrong module is
        ; still reported.
        function use_path_names_origin(this: Planner, origin: String, prefix: String, full: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; module_path_known: is `candidate` a module path, spelled absolutely
        ; or relatively (same tail rule as use_path_names_origin#)?
        function module_path_known(this: Planner, candidate: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        procedure check_use_paths(mutable this: Planner) @deep true linked

        ; ===== use-visibility measurement (stage 2, advisory) =====

        ; use_vis_count: classify a lookup_concept hit against the strict
        ; visibility rules and count. Called only under --use-vis; never
        ; changes lookup behavior.
        procedure use_vis_count(mutable this: Planner, name: String, idx: size_t) @persists true @deep true linked

        ; use_vis_visible: the strict-visibility predicate (advisory).
        ; Visible when: origin unknown (registered outside a module
        ; walk), module-local (origin file = current file), prelude
        ; (scaly stdlib package or the attached prelude module),
        ; explicit `use ns.Name`, or glob `use ns.*` whose prefix
        ; addresses the origin container.
        procedure use_vis_visible(
            mutable this: Planner,
            name: String,
            origin_file: String,
            origin_path: String
        ) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; use_vis_target_match: does a use clause with dotted prefix
        ; `prefix` import the concept whose container path is
        ; `origin_path` under `name`? Two layouts match: the concept
        ; defined directly in the container the prefix addresses
        ; (origin = prefix), and the module-named-after-concept idiom
        ; (origin = prefix + "." + name — `use scalyc.compiler.Modeler`
        ; reaching namespace Modeler inside module Modeler).
        function use_vis_target_match(
            this: Planner,
            origin_path: String,
            prefix: String,
            name: String
        ) @ret_caller true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; use_vis_origin_is_prelude: the scaly stdlib package (origin
        ; path rooted at "scaly") stays auto-visible like Rust's
        ; prelude; the prelude module itself attaches under the main
        ; module and is recognized by its file name.
        function use_vis_origin_is_prelude(
            this: Planner,
            origin_path: String,
            origin_file: String
        ) @ret_param_2 true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; ===== use-visibility enforcement (stage 4) =====

        ; use_vis_check: the enforcement predicate behind lookup_concept.
        ; Same rules as use_vis_visible, plus: LENIENT during lazy
        ; instantiation (the nested-planning trap — a generic's body is
        ; planned mid-module and must see its OWN module's names), and
        ; memoized per (concept index, current file) since lookup_concept
        ; is the hottest planner function.
        procedure use_vis_check(mutable this: Planner, name: String, idx: size_t) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; package_dir_of#: the `.../packages/<name>/` prefix of a source path,
        ; or "" for a file outside any package (a program's own modules).
        ; Diagnostic path only.
        function package_dir_of(path: String) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true @deep true returns String linked

        ; report_string_data_read#: `s.data` on a String outside the standard
        ; library. The field points at the VARINT-PREFIXED buffer, not at the
        ; characters -- `let nm "NaN"` then `takes(nm.data)` read the length
        ; byte 3 where `N` was meant, rc 0 (2026-09-24). An untyped binding of
        ; a string literal IS a String, so the spelling a Slice reader would
        ; write compiles and reads the wrong bytes. The stdlib owns the layout
        ; and is exempt.
        procedure report_string_data_read(mutable this: Planner, type_name: String, member: String, at: Span) @deep true linked

        ; basename_hash#: a deterministic number for a source file's NAME,
        ; independent of the directory it was compiled from (lambda symbols).
        function basename_hash(path: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns size_t linked

        ; lambda_arg_fits#: is argument j a lambda LITERAL whose parameter
        ; count matches the delegate `pt`? Asked in the last resolution pass
        ; only, like the other literal folds.
        procedure lambda_arg_fits(mutable this: Planner, pt: PlannedType, arg_ops: Vector[PlannedOperand], j: size_t) @ret_caller true @deep true returns bool io linked

        ; closure_for_lambda_arg#: a lambda literal handed straight to a
        ; `delegate` parameter (`apply(\a b: a + b, 3, 4)`) becomes the closure
        ; a delegate-typed binding builds (make_closure#) -- until 2026-09-24
        ; the call was "function not found": the literal planned as its body's
        ; RESULT type and matched no delegate. Null when `a` is no lambda
        ; literal this planner recorded.
        procedure closure_for_lambda_arg(mutable this: Planner, a: PlannedOperand, pt: PlannedType) @page true @direct true @stores true @deep true returns ref[PlannedOperand]? io linked

        ; generic_static_hop#: the name of the one member hop a planned Type
        ; operand of a GENERIC concept carries (`Stack[int].make`), or "" --
        ; no hop, a union variant hop, or a non-generic type (`Plain.make`
        ; goes through the s155 static path and stays there).
        function generic_static_hop(op: PlannedOperand) @page true @direct true @deep true returns String linked

        ; concept_is_constant#: is the registry entry at `idx` a module-level
        ; `define NAME: T value` (a Global definition)? Diagnostic path only.
        function concept_is_constant(this: Planner, idx: size_t) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; use_vis_hint: when `name` exists in the registry but is not
        ; visible from the current file, a "; add: use <path>" hint for
        ; the not-found diagnostic — empty otherwise. Diagnostic paths
        ; only (never hot).
        procedure use_vis_hint(mutable this: Planner, name: String) @page true @direct true @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns String linked

        ; plan_loaded_packages: extracted helper so plan_program's
        ; body stays small and a discarded plan_module# return value
        ; doesn't sit in plan_program's struct-by-value chain. The
        ; returned PlannedModule is dropped here intentionally —
        ; what we care about is the side effect of plan_function#
        ; calling record_function on every package-defined Function,
        ; which lands in instantiated_functions and is then emitted
        ; by the Emitter as both declaration and definition.
        procedure plan_loaded_packages(mutable this: Planner) @page true @stores true @deep true io linked

        ; register_sibling_programs: walk every stashed sibling
        ; Program and populate the shared registries (concepts,
        ; model_functions, model_operators) from each one. The same
        ; register_module_concepts helper is reused so nested
        ; namespaces, sub-modules, and struct-internal concepts are
        ; all flattened into the flat maps that lookup_concept /
        ; lookup_functions / lookup_operators consult.
        procedure register_sibling_programs(mutable this: Planner) @deep true linked

        ; Record a diagnostic. Callers don't need to format the message
        ; further — just describe what failed ("function not found: foo",
        ; "no matching initializer for Bar(int, String)", etc.).
        procedure add_error(mutable this: Planner, message: String) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_this_2 true @deep_whole_2 true linked

        function has_errors(this: Planner) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; Record a HARD diagnostic with the construct's source position.
        ; Use this only on paths where the construct is certain to be
        ; dropped from the IR — cli.run fails the compile on these.
        ; Said once: two checks can meet the same operand -- an unknown name in
        ; a statement that is only an expression was reported by the operand
        ; walk and again by the statement's own check (`nope + a` twice, same
        ; line and column; since 2026-09-25, seen in the REPL 2026-10-01).
        procedure add_diagnostic(mutable this: Planner, loc: Span, message: String) @persists true @deep true @deep_this_4 true @deep_whole_4 true linked

        function has_diagnostics(this: Planner) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; ====================================================================
        ; Stack guard — the measurements it rests on are at module scope
        ; (see the `scaly_stack_limit` declaration and the note above it).
        ;
        ; Byte budget for the planner's recursive descent, derived ONCE.
        ; ====================================================================
        procedure stack_budget_bytes() @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns size_t io linked

        ; Called at the top of plan_operand#, which the lldb backtrace of a real
        ; overflow shows EVERY nesting level passing through (plan_statement ->
        ; plan_if -> plan_operand -> plan_operands -> plan_action ->
        ; plan_statement): an `if` is planned as an OPERAND here, so this one
        ; site covers a long else-if chain AND a deeply parenthesized expression.
        procedure check_nesting_stack(mutable this: Planner, loc: Span) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true io linked

        ; Loud trap (exit 17), deliberately NOT an add_diagnostic: at 80 % of the
        ; limit there is no safe way to keep planning, and fabricating a stub
        ; PlannedOperand for the unwind is the "never return null after a
        ; diagnostic" hazard that already cost a SIGSEGV on the dazzle tree.
        ; Mirrors not_implemented (111) / Vector.put OOB (15) / unknown
        ; operator (14).
        function nesting_too_deep(this: Planner, loc: Span, used: size_t) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns int linked

        ; Character literals are written as double-quoted strings in a
        ; char-typed context (`let c: char "a"`, `c = "a"`, `"x" as char`).
        ; Fold a chainless STRING-constant operand into a CharacterConstant
        ; typed `char`; the string's escapes are already decoded by the
        ; Modeler, so a decoded length other than ONE byte cannot be a char —
        ; hard diagnostic instead of the old silent first-byte/garbage.
        ; Non-string operands pass through unchanged.
        procedure fold_string_operand_to_char(mutable this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand, char_type: PlannedType) @page true @direct true @deep true returns PlannedOperand linked

        ; materialize_string_operand_to_slice: turn a chainless String constant
        ; into the `Slice[elem]` VALUE its declared parameter asks for — the
        ; exact plan tree the hand-written `Slice[char](2, "as")` builds, so
        ; the emitter needs no new path and the two spellings emit the same
        ; three instructions (store length, store the plain .str pointer, load
        ; the struct).
        ;
        ; The length is the literal's DECODED byte count, which is why this
        ; must happen here and not in the emitter: `s.value` has already run
        ; the Modeler's escape decoding, so "\n" is one byte and not two. The
        ; data component is the same PlannedAs wrap coerce_string_args_to_cstr#
        ; uses, against pointer[elem] — emit_as lowers a constant String to the
        ; plain NUL-terminated .str global there, and the terminator sits one
        ; past the slice's own length, unseen by every Slice operation and
        ; available to a `data`-taking C callee.
        procedure materialize_string_operand_to_slice(
            mutable this: Planner,
            op: PlannedOperand,
            slice_type: PlannedType
        ) @page true @direct true @deep true returns PlannedOperand linked

        ; True when `op` is a chainless string-constant operand (the shape
        ; fold_string_operand_to_char converts).
        function operand_is_string_constant(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; type_is_char_like: a single-byte target (char / u8 / i8) into which a
        ; double-quoted char literal — a chainless String constant — may fold
        ; when passed as a call argument. Mirrors type_is_cstr_pointer's role
        ; for the const_char* case, but for a scalar char parameter.
        function type_is_char_like(t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; type_is_char_slice: a `Slice[char]`-family parameter — the borrowed
        ; view a string literal materialises into. It is the THIRD literal
        ; target of this family, beside type_is_char_like (a scalar char) and
        ; type_is_cstr_pointer (a bare C pointer), and the only one that is not
        ; a retype or a cast: `Slice[T]` is {length, data}, so the literal
        ; becomes a two-component CONSTRUCTION — see
        ; materialize_string_operand_to_slice#.
        ;
        ; ★Why this exists at all: without it a `Slice[char]` parameter can
        ; only be fed by writing the length out by hand at every call site
        ; (`kw_eq(buf, Slice[char](2, "as"))`), which is what kept several
        ; hundred pointer[const_char] parameters from converting — the
        ; conversion was correct and the CALL SITES were the price.
        function type_is_char_slice(t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; type_is_float_like: a floating-point target (float / f32 / double /
        ; f64) into which a bare float literal — a chainless FloatingPoint
        ; constant, default-typed f64 — may retype. The literal's double
        ; payload is kept; LLVMConstReal rounds it to the target width at
        ; emission.
        function type_is_float_like(t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; operand_is_float_constant: a chainless FloatingPoint-constant
        ; operand (the shape retype_float_operand converts).
        function operand_is_float_constant(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; retype_float_operand: restamp a bare float-literal operand's
        ; result_type to the target float width. Without this a literal in a
        ; float-typed position stays f64 and the Emitter stores DOUBLE bits
        ; into a float slot / builds a mixed float-double fcmp — invalid IR
        ; that llc swallows silently (bit garbage at runtime). Only call on
        ; operands operand_is_float_constant accepted.
        function retype_float_operand(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand, float_type: PlannedType) @page true @direct true @deep true returns PlannedOperand linked

        ; ===== integer-literal retype (the width counterpart) =====
        ;
        ; A bare integer literal is typed `int` (i64) and a bare hex literal
        ; `u64` by infer_constant_type. In a narrower integer position that
        ; width is WRONG, and the Emitter had no way to notice: `f(5)` with a
        ; u32 parameter emitted `call i32 @f(i64 5)`, and `set r.a: 9` on a
        ; u32 field emitted `store i64 9` — eight bytes over a four-byte
        ; field, silently clobbering the NEXT field. Writing `5 as u32` at
        ; every such site is what the tree did instead; these helpers make the
        ; cast unnecessary by restamping the literal to the target width.
        ;
        ; This runs strictly AFTER overload resolution, on the parameter types
        ; of the ALREADY CHOSEN callee. It therefore fixes only the width of
        ; an argument, and can never change which candidate a call picks —
        ; `append 0x2C` still resolves to append(int), not append(char).

        ; type_is_integer_like: an integer target into which a bare integer or
        ; hex literal may retype. `char` counts (it is an i8 in the emitter,
        ; and the char fold above converts only STRING literals). A generic
        ; type (`pointer[u8]`) is never a target — no name below matches one,
        ; but the guard says so outright.
        function type_is_integer_like(t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; integer_literal_fits: does the literal payload fit the target width?
        ; The payload is always non-negative (a leading minus is its OWN
        ; operand, so `-1` never reaches here as one constant). A literal that
        ; does NOT fit is left at its default width — retyping it would
        ; silently truncate, and truncation must stay something the author
        ; asked for with an explicit `as`, never something inference does.
        function integer_literal_fits(value: size_t, type_name: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; operand_is_integer_constant: a chainless Integer- or Hex-constant
        ; operand (the shape retype_integer_operand converts).
        function operand_is_integer_constant(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; operand_is_constant_integer_expression: an integer literal, or an
        ; operator over nothing but such expressions (`0 - 1`, the negative
        ; sentinel idiom). report_integer_narrowing# exempts it the way a bare
        ; literal is exempt: it names a constant, not a value that lost bits.
        function operand_is_constant_integer_expression(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; integer_operand_widens: `op` is a NON-literal integer narrower than the
        ; integer `target_type` and not a control-flow expression (a block or an
        ; arm whose leaves carry their own types). widen_integer_operand# wraps
        ; it in `as target_type` so emit_as# extends it by the SOURCE's
        ; signedness. Measured 2026-09-17 before it existed: an i32 -5 handed to
        ; an int parameter or returned from a `returns int` function arrived as
        ; 4294967291 or as 0, rc 0 — emit_call builds the callee type from the
        ; ARGUMENTS, so nothing downstream widened it at all.
        function integer_operand_widens(this: Planner, target_type: PlannedType, op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; narrow_constant_integer_operand: the one narrowing write the gate lets
        ; through without a cast -- an operator over literals (`0 - 1`) -- is
        ; wrapped in `as target` so it is STORED at the target's width, as a bare
        ; literal is retyped. Without it `set n.pos: 0 - 1` into an i32 field
        ; stored an i64 and overwrote the next field; in tscaly that was a node's
        ; payload tag, and the printer met nodes whose arm read back as nothing
        ; (2026-09-17, 231 casejs crashes).
        procedure narrow_constant_integer_operand(mutable this: Planner, target_type: PlannedType, op: PlannedOperand) @page true @direct true @deep true returns PlannedOperand linked

        procedure widen_integer_operand(mutable this: Planner, target_type: PlannedType, op: PlannedOperand) @page true @direct true @deep true returns PlannedOperand linked

        ; integer_constant_value: the payload of an operand
        ; operand_is_integer_constant accepted. Zero for anything else — the
        ; callers gate on the predicate first.
        function integer_constant_value(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @ret_param_1 true @deep true returns size_t linked

        ; operand_retypes_to_integer: the whole gate in one predicate — is
        ; `op` a bare integer/hex literal whose payload fits integer target
        ; `t`? Callers that answer true then call retype_integer_operand.
        function operand_retypes_to_integer(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand, t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; retype_integer_operand: restamp a bare integer-literal operand's
        ; result_type to the target integer width, so emit_constant builds the
        ; constant at the width the store or the callee reads. Only call on
        ; operands operand_retypes_to_integer accepted.
        function retype_integer_operand(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand, int_type: PlannedType) @page true @direct true @deep true returns PlannedOperand linked

        ; type_is_integer_64: an integer type that is 64 bits wide. Retyping a
        ; bare literal to one of these can never narrow anything, which is what
        ; makes the ARITHMETIC retype safe for them and unsafe for the rest.
        function type_is_integer_64(t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; is_integer_arithmetic_operator: the arithmetic and bitwise operators
        ; a bare literal may retype across when the OTHER side is 64 bits wide
        ; (type_is_integer_64). Two things ride on this:
        ;
        ;   * signedness. `is_signed` in emit_intrinsic_binary_op derives from
        ;     the LEFT operand's type alone, so `256 / count` with count a
        ;     size_t emitted `sdiv` — the literal's `int` was on the left and
        ;     won. Retyping the literal to size_t makes it `udiv`. Measured:
        ;     that literal-on-the-left shape is the ONLY one that flipped;
        ;     `count / 2`, `this.vec_size / 2` and `(hi - lo) / 2` were all
        ;     already unsigned.
        ;   * nothing else. At 64 bits the literal's own width is already the
        ;     operand's width, so no instruction changes except the predicate.
        ;
        ; A NARROW other side is excluded on purpose: there the retype would
        ; narrow the RESULT too (`x + 1` on a u32 would wrap at 2^32 instead of
        ; widening), and that is a change of meaning rather than a fix.
        function is_integer_arithmetic_operator(name: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; is_integer_comparison_operator: the relational and equality
        ; operators — the only binary operators where retyping a bare integer
        ; literal to the OTHER side's type is purely a fix. The result is a
        ; bool either way, so nothing downstream changes type; what changes is
        ; the predicate. `x < 0` with x unsigned used to emit `icmp slt`,
        ; because the literal's `int` won the pair and int is signed — and slt
        ; differs from ult exactly when the top bit is set.
        ;
        ; The ARITHMETIC operators are deliberately excluded. There, narrowing
        ; the literal would also narrow the RESULT: `x + 1` with x: u32 is an
        ; i64 add today (the typed side widens) and would become an i32 add
        ; that wraps at 2^32. That is a change of meaning, not a correction,
        ; and it is not this pass's business — the explicit cast stays the way
        ; to ask for narrow arithmetic.
        function is_integer_comparison_operator(name: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; arg_is_foldable_float: is the argument at position `j` a bare float
        ; literal that may retype to a float/f32 parameter? The counterpart to
        ; arg_is_foldable_char_string for the float-width case.
        function arg_is_foldable_float(this: Planner, arg_ops: Vector[PlannedOperand], j: size_t) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; arg_is_foldable_char_string: is the argument at position `j` (in the
        ; parallel arg-operand list gathered at the call site) a chainless
        ; String constant that may fold to a char/u8 parameter? A short/empty
        ; arg_ops list — the default at call sites that do not opt in — yields
        ; false, so the char-fold match is byte-identical to the pre-feature
        ; matchers everywhere it is not explicitly enabled.
        function arg_is_foldable_char_string(this: Planner, arg_ops: Vector[PlannedOperand], j: size_t) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; operand_is_null_constant: is the operand the bare `null` literal?
        ; Used to EXCLUDE null comparisons from the equality desugar —
        ; `x <> null` is an NPO presence test, never a content compare.
        function operand_is_null_constant(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; ================================================================
        ; Flow narrowing: a null test PROVES the name non-null in the branch
        ; it guards, so that branch may read it as the plain reference.
        ;
        ; Why it exists: arming option_arg_into_plain_ref# on the untouched
        ; tree reports 2459 sites, and 1222 of them sit behind exactly such a
        ; test. Those 1222 are not defects -- they are the planner unable to
        ; read a guard the author already wrote. Without narrowing the gate's
        ; price is an `as ref[T]` unwrap at every one of them.
        ;
        ; ★★★It must not change what EXISTING code means, and the first
        ; attempt did: narrowing turned `*x` inside a guarded branch into a
        ; hard rc-4 (report_deref_of_ref#), and sweeping those stars away
        ; rewrote 676 UNTYPED bindings from a COPY into an ALIAS -- `let v
        ; (*p)` copies, `let v p` aliases. Only three of the 676 broke loudly.
        ; So the star STAYS legal on a narrowed binding: the declaration is
        ; still `ref[T]?`, the author's deref is still honest, and narrowing
        ; only ADDS the ability to pass the name on directly.
        ;
        ; ★★★A `var` narrows too, and the bug that made it look impossible was
        ; in the SHADOW, not in the idea. The first attempt hardcoded
        ; `is_mutable: false` on the pushed binding -- harmless while only
        ; immutable names narrowed, since theirs was false anyway. For a `var`
        ; it meant every later `set x: ...` found a NON-mutable target, and the
        ; emitter's Variable arm returns WITHOUT STORING when the target is not
        ; mutable. Every assignment after a guard became a silent no-op: the
        ; compiler built, compiled every package, reported nothing, and emitted
        ; NO OUTPUT for 234 of 243 regressions. The write through
        ; lookup_local_binding#'s reference was never the culprit --
        ; `optional_ref_member_store.scaly` had pinned that shape for weeks.
        ;
        ; SOUNDNESS, and it is what makes a `var` narrowable at all: the
        ; proof holds from the test until the name is ASSIGNED, and
        ; plan_statements# plans in PROGRAM ORDER, so clearing the fact when
        ; it meets `set x: ...` is exactly the right lifetime --
        ;     if x <> null { f(x)   set x: null   g(x) }
        ; narrows f's argument and not g's. A `while` body needs no special
        ; case for the back edge: the condition is re-tested at the top of
        ; every iteration, so the proof is live again exactly where the body
        ; starts, which is where the walk restores it.
        ; ================================================================

        ; The payload of an NPO Option, when that payload is pointer-shaped.
        ; Ask the SHAPE, not the name: `ref[T]?` and `pointer[T]?` both arrive
        ; as an Option wrapper, and a name test that knows only "ref" is the
        ; standing bug of this seam.
        procedure npo_option_payload(t: PlannedType, mutable out: ref[PlannedType]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_into_1_1 true returns bool linked

        ; narrow_null_test#: does this condition compare a BARE local against
        ; null, and which branch does it prove? `x <> null` proves the
        ; CONSEQUENT, `x = null` the ALTERNATIVE -- and, when the consequent
        ; leaves the block, everything after the statement.
        ;
        ; A member chain is deliberately not read: `a.b <> null` proves
        ; something about a FIELD, and a scope shadow can only narrow a name.
        procedure narrow_null_test(
            this: Planner,
            mutable cond: PlannedOperand,
            mutable out_name: ref[String],
            mutable out_positive: ref[bool]
        ) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; narrow_null_test_path#: the same test, read for a member CHAIN.
        ; `if this.chunk <> null` proves a PLACE, not a name -- so the fact is
        ; keyed by the dotted PATH and carries the chain's own type, which is
        ; the one thing a name-keyed shadow cannot supply. See
        ; define_narrowed_path#.
        procedure narrow_null_test_path(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable cond: PlannedOperand,
            mutable out_path: ref[String],
            mutable out_type: ref[PlannedType],
            mutable out_positive: ref[bool]
        ) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_into_3_2 true returns bool linked

        ; operand_path#: the dotted PATH a member chain spells, when every hop
        ; is a PROPERTY of a bare local -- `this.chunk`, `b.syntax`. A METHOD
        ; hop ends it, and must: `x.f().y` names a fresh value, where narrowing
        ; is a statement about a PLACE. A union payload hop likewise names
        ; something the tag decides, not a field.
        procedure operand_path(mutable this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand, mutable out_path: ref[String]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; The destructuring both readers share: which side of an `=`/`<>`
        ; against null is the SUBJECT, and which branch does the test prove?
        ; Split out when the path reader landed -- one copy of this is enough,
        ; and the two readers differ only in what they accept as a subject.
        procedure narrow_null_subject(
            this: Planner,
            cond: PlannedOperand,
            mutable out_subject: ref[PlannedOperand],
            mutable out_positive: ref[bool]
        ) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; Shadow `name` with its non-optional payload. lookup_local_binding#
        ; scans innermost-first, so the shadow wins until the scope holding it
        ; is popped -- which is also what un-narrows it, for free. Answers
        ; whether it pushed one.
        procedure define_narrowed(mutable this: Planner, mutable name: String) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_this_2 true @deep_whole_2 true returns bool linked

        ; ================================================================
        ; FIELD narrowing: the fact is keyed by the dotted PATH.
        ;
        ; `if this.chunk <> null ... f(this.chunk)` is the ordinary shape of
        ; the ports and worth 277 of the sites. define_narrowed# cannot serve
        ; it: a scope SHADOW can only narrow a NAME, and `this.chunk` is not
        ; one. So the path goes into the same scope_bindings table under its
        ; own dotted key -- which no identifier can collide with, because a
        ; Scaly name is alphanumeric -- and push_scope#/pop_scope# then give
        ; the fact exactly the lifetime a shadow would have had, for free.
        ;
        ; ★The entry carries the CHAIN's type, not a binding's: there is no
        ; binding to copy one from, and the type is what npo_option_payload#
        ; is asked about.
        ;
        ; ★★★INVALIDATION is the whole risk, and it is deliberately blunt:
        ; every statement clears every path fact (clear_path_narrowings#,
        ; from retire_narrowing#, which plan_statements# runs after EACH
        ; statement). A field is reachable through any alias of its object,
        ; and this planner has no alias analysis -- so anything finer would be
        ; a claim it cannot make. The cost is real and stated rather than
        ; hidden: only the FIRST statement of a guarded block is judged
        ; against the proof, and a second use of the same field reports again.
        ; The direction is the safe one: report rather than stay silent.
        ; ================================================================

        ; Record the fact for a dotted path. Answers whether it pushed one.
        procedure define_narrowed_path(mutable this: Planner, path: String, mutable t: PlannedType) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_this_6 true @deep_whole_6 true returns bool linked

        function path_is_narrowed(this: Planner, path: String) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; Clear every path fact. A dotted key is the marker: an identifier
        ; cannot contain a dot, so a key that does is one of ours.
        procedure clear_path_narrowings(mutable this: Planner) @deep true linked

        ; ★46, not `"." as char`: String.get answers a u8, and comparing one
        ; against a character literal is the shape that crashes llc with "Do
        ; not know how to promote this operator's operand!" -- a bootstrap link
        ; failure two steps later. The tree writes such tests numerically.
        function name_is_path(name: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; ================================================================
        ; The BRANCH JOIN, and it is the first dataflow this narrowing owes.
        ;
        ; plan_if# plans the consequent and the alternative in the SAME scope,
        ; so without a join an assignment in one branch retires the narrowing
        ; for the other -- and the two branches are exclusive, so nothing the
        ; consequent assigns is true on the alternative's path:
        ;     if cur = node { set cur: null } else NumberCache.advance(order, cur)
        ; reported on the `else`, where nothing was assigned at all
        ; (dazzle/NumberCache.scaly:289). Worth 108 sites.
        ;
        ; Three steps, and the third is what makes it a JOIN rather than a
        ; reset: SNAPSHOT the facts before the consequent, RESTORE them before
        ; the alternative, then RE-APPLY the consequent's retirements at the
        ; end. A name survives the `if` only if NEITHER branch assigned it,
        ; which is the conservative direction on both edges.
        ;
        ; ★It works on scope_bindings INDICES, not on names. retire_narrowing#
        ; clears the flag IN PLACE and never pops, so an index names the same
        ; binding throughout -- where a name answers about whichever shadow is
        ; innermost NOW, which need not be the one that was cleared.
        ; ================================================================

        ; The live narrowing facts, as indices into scope_bindings. The floor
        ; is scope_isolation_base for the reason lookup_local_binding#'s is:
        ; below it the bindings belong to the enclosing function.
        procedure collect_narrowed(this: Planner, mutable acc: ref[Array[size_t]]) @deep true linked

        ; Set the fact on every index in `idx`. Bounds-guarded: a branch pops
        ; the scopes it pushed, and an index past the end would name a binding
        ; that no longer exists.
        procedure set_narrowed_at(mutable this: Planner, idx: Array[size_t], on: bool) @deep true linked

        ; Which of `snap` did the branch just planned RETIRE? Those are the
        ; ones the join re-clears once the OTHER branch has been planned
        ; against the restored facts.
        procedure collect_retired(this: Planner, snap: Array[size_t], mutable acc: ref[Array[size_t]]) @deep true linked

        ; ===== glob-ambiguity diagnostic (use-visibility, hard) =====
        ;
        ; Two glob imports in one file (`use a.*` and `use b.*`) that BOTH
        ; supply a concept of the same name make a bare reference to that
        ; name ambiguous: which concept it binds would depend on the
        ; registry's last-write-wins order, not on the source. That is a
        ; hard error at the use site. An explicit `use a.Name` in the same
        ; file names the winner and suppresses the error (explicit > glob).
        ; Runs once after registration, only when enforcement is on; the
        ; tree carries no such collision (the lexer/Syntax Literal families
        ; were unified), so this fires only on a genuine new clash.

        ; use_vis_file_has_explicit: does file `f` carry a non-glob use
        ; clause naming `name` (which disambiguates a glob collision)?
        function use_vis_file_has_explicit(this: Planner, f: String, name: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; file_has_explicit_cached: O(this-file's-explicit-uses) replacement
        ; for use_vis_file_has_explicit(file, name) on lookup_concept's hot
        ; path. A PURE READ — the list is populated incrementally in
        ; record_module_uses (a persisting context; lookup_concept's `this` is
        ; effectively a copy so a mutation THERE is lost). `file` is fixed
        ; after init, so the cached list covers the whole compile.
        function file_has_explicit_cached(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; origin_matches_explicit_use: does some EXPLICIT (non-"*") use clause
        ; `use P.name` in the CURRENT file name an origin matching origin_path?
        ; Reuses use_vis_target_match (origin = P, or P.name — the module-named-
        ; after-concept idiom). Drives glob-type-disambiguation in lookup_concept.
        function origin_matches_explicit_use(this: Planner, name: String, origin_path: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; lookup_concept_explicit_scan: scan `concepts` BACKWARD for a same-named
        ; entry whose ORIGIN matches an explicit use clause for `name` in the
        ; current file. Backward so the s95 same-file namespace+struct pair still
        ; yields the later Structure entry (both share the file's origin, so both
        ; match; the last one wins). Only called from lookup_concept when the
        ; last-write-wins entry does NOT match the explicit use — the genuine
        ; glob-ambiguity case (`use a.Widget` while a glob also pulls b.Widget).
        function lookup_concept_explicit_scan(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Concept]? linked

        ; use_vis_file_glob_supplies: does a glob clause in `f` import the
        ; concept named `name` from container `origin`?
        function use_vis_file_glob_supplies(this: Planner, f: String, name: String, origin: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; check_glob_ambiguities: the enforcement entry, called once from
        ; plan_program. For each distinct file that declared a glob, and
        ; each concept name, count the DISTINCT origin modules that supply
        ; it into that file via a glob (skipping same-file and prelude
        ; origins, and names an explicit use already disambiguates). Two or
        ; more distinct glob origins for one name is a hard diagnostic.
        procedure check_glob_ambiguities(mutable this: Planner) @deep true linked

        procedure check_file_glob_ambiguities(mutable this: Planner, f: String, heads: ref[Array[size_t]], next: ref[Array[size_t]]) @deep true linked

        ; Look up a previously loaded package in the cache.
        function lookup_loaded_package(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Module]? linked

        ; Phase-1 lambda registry: record the model lambda bound to `name`.
        procedure record_lambda_model(mutable this: Planner, name: String, lam: Lambda) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_this_6 true @deep_whole_6 true linked

        ; Return the lambda model bound to `name`, or null. Forward scan
        ; (first binding wins) — Phase-1 lambdas are not scope-shadowed.
        function lookup_lambda_model(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Lambda]? linked

        ; Look up a previously loaded intra-package module by its full
        ; file path.
        function lookup_loaded_module_path(this: Planner, path: String) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Module]? linked

        ; Compose "base/<name>/<version>/<name>.scaly" using Path.join#.
        function build_package_file_path(
            this: Planner,
            base: String,
            name: String,
            version: String
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true @deep true returns String linked

        ; register_loaded_package: pre-populate the package cache.
        ; Callers (cli.scaly, test harnesses) parse a package source
        ; file externally, build a Model.Module for it, then hand
        ; the root Module here keyed by package name.
        ;
        ; This is the pre-parsed-cache wiring: the Scaly Planner
        ; can't drive the Scaly Parser + Modeler from its own body
        ; yet, so the orchestration of "find the .scaly file, parse
        ; it, model it" happens one level up. The Planner just asks
        ; "do you have package X for me?" via load_package_on_demand#.
        procedure register_loaded_package(mutable this: Planner, name: String, mod: Module) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_this_6 true @deep_whole_6 true linked

        ; register_loaded_module: pre-populate the intra-package
        ; module cache keyed by full file path. Same contract as
        ; register_loaded_package — parsing + modelling happens
        ; outside the Planner; the Planner consults this cache when
        ; a `use` statement references a peer module file.
        procedure register_loaded_module(mutable this: Planner, path: String, mod: Module) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_this_6 true @deep_whole_6 true linked

        ; load_package_on_demand: cache-only lookup of a package by
        ; name. Returns the pre-registered root Module if one is
        ; cached, otherwise null. Pre-registration happens via
        ; register_loaded_package. The filesystem probing helper
        ; build_package_file_path# is retained for callers that want
        ; to discover a candidate file before parsing it externally.
        function load_package_on_demand(
            this: Planner,
            name: String
        ) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Module]? linked

        ; load_intra_package_module: cache-only lookup of a peer
        ; module inside an already-mounted package, keyed by the
        ; full file path ("BasePath/ModuleName.scaly"). Callers
        ; pre-populate via register_loaded_module.
        function load_intra_package_module(
            this: Planner,
            base_path: String,
            module_name: String
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Module]? linked

        ; ================================================================
        ; Model-function and operator registries.
        ; ================================================================

        procedure register_function(mutable this: Planner, f: Function) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_this_2 true @deep_whole_2 true linked

        ; function_origin_file: the file the registered function `f` (the same
        ; name AND the same span) was declared in, or "" when unknown. A span
        ; carries only offsets, so the name is needed to tell two files apart.
        function function_origin_file(this: Planner, f: Function) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true @deep true returns String linked

        procedure register_operator(mutable this: Planner, o: Operator) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_this_2 true @deep_whole_2 true linked

        ; lookup_functions: return every Function in the registry whose
        ; name matches. Overloads are preserved — the caller picks by
        ; argument-type compatibility.
        function lookup_functions(this: Planner, name: String) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true @deep true returns Vector[Function] linked

        ; lookup_functions_preferring#: the candidates for a BARE-name call, the
        ; ones the call can mean first. A member of a namespace is visible by
        ; its bare name inside that namespace (a sibling) or through a `use`
        ; alias; from anywhere else it is mangled as a FREE function, a symbol
        ; nothing defines. So when such a call also has a visible candidate --
        ; a free function, the program's own included -- the namespace members
        ; of other namespaces drop out: a program's `run()` binds to its own
        ; `run`, not to a package's `util.run` (2026-09-25; it bound to
        ; `util.run` and linked to the program's `run` only because both came
        ; out `_Z3runv`). With no visible candidate the list is unchanged.
        function lookup_functions_preferring(this: Planner, name: String) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true @deep true returns Vector[Function] linked

        function lookup_operators(this: Planner, name: String) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true @deep true returns Vector[Operator] linked

        function is_function(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; True iff `name` is a declared operator (in the operator-symbol
        ; registry). Used to give text-named operators a precedence and, in
        ; stage-0, to recognise them in infix position.
        function is_operator(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; ================================================================
        ; register_runtime_functions: synthesize Function entries for the
        ; C runtime functions the RBMM lowering needs (aligned_alloc,
        ; free, exit, …). Matches Planner.cpp's static table.
        ;
        ; Idempotent — call as many times as desired; the registry is
        ; appended to, and duplicates are tolerated by lookup_functions
        ; (it walks every entry anyway).
        ;
        ; Parameter items and return types are built with Type.name
        ; Vectors so resolve_type_base recognises them correctly.
        ; ================================================================
        procedure register_runtime_functions(mutable this: Planner) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true linked

        ; ================================================================
        ; Method / initializer / property lookup on user types.
        ; ================================================================

        ; collect_methods_in: append every Function of the members Vector whose
        ; name matches, in member order. Shared by lookup_methods across
        ; Structure and Union variants; answered from the list's method table.
        procedure collect_methods_in(
            mutable this: Planner,
            members: Vector[Member],
            method_name: String,
            mutable acc: ref[Array[Function]]
        ) @deep true linked

        ; The method table of a member list (MemberIndex), built at its first
        ; ask; its number, or -1 for an empty list.
        procedure member_table(mutable this: Planner, members: Vector[Member]) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns i64 linked

        ; Enter a list address -> table number; past half load the map doubles.
        procedure member_map_put(mutable this: Planner, key: u64, id: size_t) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true linked

        procedure member_map_insert(mutable this: Planner, key: u64, id: size_t) @deep true linked

        procedure lookup_methods(
            mutable this: Planner,
            struct_name: String,
            method_name: String
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true @deep true returns Vector[Function] linked

        ; lookup_property: find a struct field by name. Returns a
        ; reference into the Structure's properties Vector — valid as
        ; long as the underlying Concept is live in the registry.
        procedure lookup_property(
            mutable this: Planner,
            struct_name: String,
            property_name: String
        ) @deep true returns ref[Property]? linked

        ; reorder_named_components#: give a NAMED tuple component its meaning.
        ;
        ; ★★★ The grammar has carried the name since the beginning -- `Component`
        ; is `Operand+ [':' Operand+]` and `Modeler.handle_component#` puts it in
        ; `Model.Component.name` -- and the construction path IGNORED it, filling
        ; positionally. Measured before this landed: `Rec(gamma: 3, alpha: 1,
        ; beta: 2)` on `define Rec (alpha, beta, gamma)` answered `3 1 2` at rc 0
        ; with nothing said. Worse than a wrong value: `check_one_component_type#`
        ; RETURNS EARLY on a named component -- exactly because the name meant
        ; nothing -- so the labelled spelling also switched OFF the type check
        ; that would have caught it. A spelling that looks like documentation and
        ; silently disables a gate is the worst shape this compiler has.
        ;
        ; What it does: when ANY component carries a name, resolve every name to
        ; a property index and return the components in DECLARATION order, with
        ; the names STRIPPED so the positional check downstream applies to them
        ; like any other. No name anywhere -> the input unchanged, so every
        ; existing construction in the tree emits byte-identically.
        ;
        ; ★ The rules are deliberately strict, because each looser one has a
        ; silent failure behind it:
        ;   * name one field and you must name them ALL -- a mixed list would
        ;     make the reader compute which positions the labels shifted.
        ;   * an unknown name is an error, never a positional fallback.
        ;   * a field named twice is an error; the last-wins rule would make the
        ;     first value's side effects invisible.
        ;   * the named fields must be a PREFIX of the declaration. A trailing
        ;     omission is what fill_default_components# fills; a MIDDLE one it
        ;     cannot, and inventing a value here would be the uninitialised-field
        ;     class this path already exists to prevent.
        ; ★ The body constructs nothing itself -- it only forwards to
        ; place_named_components# -- so page-inference rule 1
        ; (`body_may_allocate`) answers NO, and without a caller page the
        ; vector the callee builds lands on THIS routine's frame, released at
        ; the return (measured: TWO named constructions in one file segfaulted
        ; the planner, rc 139). It was a DECLARED `rp` until 2026-09-25; the
        ; page comes from R7 now, which follows a returned local through the
        ; `set` that fills it (ret_page_stmts_sets#). The mangled name is where
        ; it shows: `PN4scaly6memory4PageE`, as on restamp_components#.
        procedure reorder_named_components(
            mutable this: Planner,
            struct_name: String,
            loc: Span,
            provided: ref[Vector[PlannedComponent]]?,
            ctor_type: PlannedType
        ) @page true @returnpage true @deep true returns ref[Vector[PlannedComponent]]? linked

        ; The Structure half of reorder_named_components#, split out so the
        ; caller stays one statement per rule (the s178 alloca-pool discipline).
        procedure place_named_components(
            mutable this: Planner,
            struct_name: String,
            s: Structure,
            loc: Span,
            pv: Vector[PlannedComponent],
            named: size_t
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true @deep true returns ref[Vector[PlannedComponent]]? linked

        ; fill_default_components: for a data-class construction that matched
        ; no explicit init#, append default-value components for every
        ; property beyond the provided count (`Config()` with all-defaulted
        ; fields, or `Config(true)` filling the trailing default). Mirrors
        ; stage-0 Planner.cpp:9229-9275. Returns a fresh component vector when
        ; defaults were appended, else the input unchanged. Only fires when
        ; the construction genuinely omitted trailing properties that ALL have
        ; defaults; a full construction and a concept whose missing properties
        ; lack defaults are both no-ops (keeps compiler-own emission unchanged
        ; where every construction provides all args → fixpoint-safe).
        procedure fill_default_components(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable struct_name: String,
            loc: Span,
            provided: ref[Vector[PlannedComponent]]?,
            ctor_type: PlannedType,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType]
        ) @page true @direct true @stores true @deep true @deep_this_8 true returns ref[Vector[PlannedComponent]]? io linked

        ; find_initializer: locate an Initializer on a Struct-shaped
        ; concept whose parameter types are pairwise compatible with
        ; `arg_types`. Returns null when no match. Deliberately linear —
        ; matching against the first compatible overload.
        ; check_initializer_match: returns the Initializer pointer if
        ; its input matches arg_types (after skipping any implicit
        ; page parameter, which is already excluded from input by the
        ; Modeler). `exact_only` selects types_equal vs types_compatible.
        procedure check_initializer_match(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable ini: Initializer,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            exact_only: bool
        ) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_this_2 true returns bool io linked

        ; (The former concept_is_heap_backed NAME ALLOWLIST lived here. It is
        ; gone: residency is now DERIVED from the concept's own bodies by
        ; concept_needs_this_residency below — see its comment. Vector and
        ; String are resident too now: their inits derive the buffer's page
        ; from the value's own address. Neither mutates through it after
        ; construction, so a record holding one by value stays a stack value
        ; -- structure_fields_need_residency#.)

        ; concept_needs_this_residency: true iff the named concept derives a
        ; page from its own address anywhere in its bodies — a `^this`
        ; construction (Type or Matrix life) or a `Page.get(this ...)` call,
        ; in any init, method, operator, or the deinit. Such an instance MUST
        ; live on a page; a stack instance would compute a garbage page
        ; header. SOURCE-DRIVEN: this replaced the concept_is_heap_backed
        ; name allowlist (Array/List/builders/HashSet/HashMap/StringBuilder
        ; all classify via their own bodies — Array/builders through the
        ; two-step `let own_page Page.get(this as pointer[void])`, List
        ; through `Node[T]^this` in add, HashSet/HashMap/StringBuilder
        ; through generic `^this` inits). Consulted on the fall-through
        ; construction paths (Unspecified life) in collapse_operand_sequence;
        ; explicit #/^ constructions never reach it.
        ; ================================================================
        ; --emit-interface (Interface.scaly): per routine of the ROOT package
        ; what a bodyless declaration must say that the planner derives from
        ; its body -- the caller page (R1..R7, part of the mangled name),
        ; whether it stores an argument beyond its frame, the page sets it
        ; joins, its return residence and its deep persist masks, rendered
        ; here as the attributes the interface writes after its parameter
        ; list -- and per concept whether a construction of it must be
        ; page-resident. One fact per declaration, keyed by file and source
        ; offset; Interface does the text surgery.
        ; ================================================================

        procedure interface_facts(mutable this: Planner, m: Module, mutable facts: ref[Array[InterfaceFact]]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_pageof_4 true io linked

        procedure facts_module(mutable this: Planner, m: Module, generic: bool, mutable facts: ref[Array[InterfaceFact]]) @page true @direct true @deep true @deep_pageof_8 true @deep_into_3_2 true io linked

        function fact(tag: char, file: String, start: size_t, end: size_t, kind: char, name: String, generic: bool, resident: bool, inplace: bool, text: String) @page true @direct true @deep true returns InterfaceFact linked

        procedure facts_members(mutable this: Planner, file: String, members: Vector[Member], generic: bool, mutable facts: ref[Array[InterfaceFact]]) @page true @direct true @deep true @deep_whole_2 true @deep_pageof_16 true @deep_into_4_2 true io linked

        procedure facts_function(mutable this: Planner, file: String, f: Function, generic: bool, mutable facts: ref[Array[InterfaceFact]]) @page true @direct true @deep true @deep_whole_2 true @deep_pageof_16 true @deep_into_4_6 true io linked

        ; `@deep true` and the deep persist masks of a body that are not zero
        ; -- beyond, this, whole, pageof and the fifteen into-parameter slots
        ; (bit 0 is `this`) -- for a linked caller's deep gate; a missing mask
        ; is zero
        procedure facts_deep_attributes(mutable this: Planner, f: Function) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true @deep true returns String linked

        procedure facts_concept(mutable this: Planner, file: String, c: Concept, generic: bool, mutable facts: ref[Array[InterfaceFact]]) @page true @direct true @deep true @deep_whole_2 true @deep_pageof_16 true @deep_into_4_6 true io linked

        ; structure_init_stores_build#: an `init` that puts a value it BUILDS
        ; into a field of the object under construction (`set name: String(t)`)
        ; puts it on the page of that object -- plan_action#'s store rule 3
        ; places it with `^this`, the init having no caller page -- so the
        ; object must live on a page (2026-09-25; on the stack Page.get(this)
        ; answered a page below the frame). A zero-argument construction
        ; builds nothing and does not count (ops_build_page_value#).
        procedure structure_init_stores_build(mutable this: Planner, s: Structure) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; a `set <field>: <build>` anywhere in this action: in a block, an arm
        ; of if/choose/match, a loop body
        procedure action_stores_build_into(mutable this: Planner, a: Action, fields: Array[String], params: Array[String]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        procedure stmt_stores_build_into(mutable this: Planner, st: Statement, fields: Array[String], params: Array[String]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        procedure ops_store_build_into(mutable this: Planner, ops: Vector[Operand], fields: Array[String], params: Array[String]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; instantiated_type_needs_residency#: concept_needs_this_residency for a
        ; construction whose TYPE ARGUMENTS are known. A generic record holding
        ; a `T` by value is resident when its `T` is one that allocates through
        ; its own address -- `Box[Reg]` with `Reg` holding an Array: the field
        ; residency walk sees only `T` at model level, so the Box was laid on
        ; the stack and `b.inner.names.add(...)` asked Page.get of a stack slot
        ; (exit 19, 2026-09-25). Each field typed by a type parameter is asked
        ; about its argument.
        procedure instantiated_type_needs_residency(mutable this: Planner, name: String, t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        procedure concept_needs_this_residency(mutable this: Planner, name: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; structure_fields_need_residency#: a record holding a page-resident
        ; value BY VALUE (`define Bag (var items: Array[int])`) is resident
        ; itself: the field's methods derive their page from the field's
        ; address, which is inside the record. Built on the stack, `put(&bag,
        ; 5)` grew the Array through Page.get of a stack address -- right by
        ; luck, rc 139 under SCALY_POISON (2026-09-25). References, pointers
        ; and options hold no value and are skipped. ONLY a field type whose
        ; NON-init members allocate through `this` counts (Array.add,
        ; StringBuilder.append): an init runs on the value's own storage and
        ; the field receives a copy, so a String field -- whose Page.get(this)
        ; sits in its inits alone -- leaves the record a stack value. Counting
        ; the inits too put every PlannedType on the frame page and cost the
        ; compiler +19 % peak (measured 2026-09-25).
        procedure structure_fields_need_residency(mutable this: Planner, s: Structure, depth: size_t) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; structure_is_resident#: carried into the plan as
        ; PlannedStructure.resident, so the Emitter can refuse to put such a
        ; construction on the stack when the page it was told to use does not
        ; exist in the routine (region_alloc_life#).
        procedure structure_is_resident(mutable this: Planner, s: Structure, name: String) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; structure_inplace_residency#: the concept allocates through its OWN
        ; address after construction -- a member body does, or a field held
        ; by value does -- so whatever holds it by value must be resident.
        ; The interface states it as `@inplace` (bodies are linked there).
        procedure structure_inplace_residency(mutable this: Planner, s: Structure, depth: size_t) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        procedure field_type_needs_residency(mutable this: Planner, name: String, depth: size_t) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        function structure_members_have_this_alloc(this: Planner, s: Structure) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        function structure_init_has_this_alloc(this: Planner, s: Structure) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        function impl_has_this_alloc(this: Planner, impl: Implementation) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        function action_has_this_alloc(this: Planner, a: Action) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        function operands_have_this_alloc(this: Planner, ops: Vector[Operand]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        function operand_is_page_get(op: Operand) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; True when the operand is the argument shape of a `this`-receiving
        ; call: a Tuple whose first component's value leads with `this`
        ; (`Page.get(this as pointer[void])`), or a bare `this` (parenless).
        function operand_leads_with_this(op: Operand) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        function operand_is_bare_this(op: Operand) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        function type_is_bare_this(ty: Type) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; Exhaustive Expression walker, mirroring operand_references_rp /
        ; operand_has_call_life below: a `^this` construction or a
        ; `Page.get(this)` call nested inside a control-flow operand
        ; (if/choose/match/for/while/repeat/try/lambda) must be visible, else
        ; the concept silently classifies stack-safe and bare constructions
        ; stack-alloc an instance whose Page.get(this) then reads a garbage
        ; page header.
        function operand_has_this_alloc(this: Planner, op: Operand) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        function tuple_has_this_alloc(this: Planner, tp: Tuple) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        function block_has_this_alloc(this: Planner, bk: Block) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        function matrix_has_this_alloc(this: Planner, mx: Matrix) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        function statement_has_this_alloc(this: Planner, st: Statement) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        function type_life_is_this(t: Type) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        function lifetime_is_this(lt: Lifetime) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        procedure find_initializer(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable struct_name: String,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType]
        ) @page true @direct true @deep true returns ref[Initializer]? io linked

        ; check_initializer_option_match#: the compatible match, except that an
        ; Option around a reference also matches a `ref` parameter of the same
        ; pointee -- at least one argument must need it. The argument gate at
        ; the construction (report_null_into_ref_arg#) then judges the OPERAND:
        ; a name a null test has proven passes (define_narrowed#), anything
        ; else is told to unwrap. Before 2026-09-24 the init was refused and a
        ; guarded `Holder(o)` reported "property n would be left
        ; uninitialized", the positional-tuple path's message.
        procedure check_initializer_option_match(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable ini: Initializer,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType]
        ) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_this_2 true returns bool io linked

        ; get_property_index: position of a named property in the struct.
        ; Returns -1 if the type isn't a struct or the property is missing.
        procedure get_property_index(
            mutable this: Planner,
            struct_name: String,
            property_name: String
        ) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns int linked

        ; variant_payload_points_at: does a POINTER-SHAPED variant payload
        ; point at `variant_name`? A payload declared `ref[X]` / `pointer[X]`
        ; carries the WRAPPER in its own `name`, so the `when v: X` spelling —
        ; the one every by-value arm uses — matched nothing, lookup_variant_tag
        ; answered -1 and plan_when left the tag at 0. Every such arm then
        ; dispatched to variant 0 in silence: measured on a two-arm union over
        ; ref payloads, `when a: RecA` took arm 0 and `when b: RecB` fell
        ; through to the else, rc 0, no diagnostic. The B3 guard in plan_when
        ; cannot see it either — it exempts an arm name that is itself a
        ; concept, and X always is.
        ; ★The two arms of this shape already in the tree (dazzle's
        ; Insn.MakeFlowObj, opensp's MessageArg.Vec) are unaffected: both are
        ; written with the arm's FIELD name, which always resolved.
        ; ★Asked only AFTER a full pass with the old rules has found nothing,
        ; so every `when` that resolves today resolves to the SAME arm and
        ; emission does not move.
        ; ★`Option` is deliberately NOT unwrapped: an optional payload is a
        ; different value space from the value it may hold, and `when v: X`
        ; over `V: X?` would bind something that can be absent.
        function variant_payload_points_at(
            v_type: ref[scalyc.compiler.Model.Type],
            variant_name: String
        ) @deep true returns bool linked

        ; lookup_variant: find a union variant by name and return its tag.
        ; Returns -1 if the concept is missing or not a Union, or if the
        ; variant name is not present.
        procedure lookup_variant_tag(
            mutable this: Planner,
            union_name: String,
            variant_name: String
        ) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns int linked

        ; lookup_variant_tag_by_name: lookup_variant_tag restricted to the
        ; variant's own FIELD name, never its payload TYPE name.
        ;
        ; The type-name arm above exists for `choose … when TypeName: x`, which
        ; matches by the payload type. Reading a CONSTRUCTION that way is dead
        ; code in every well-formed program — if the payload record is visible,
        ; lookup_concept has already resolved the name as a struct ctor — and it
        ; was live in exactly one broken one: a record the file cannot SEE.
        ; `U.First(Rec(11, 22, 33))` with `Rec` hidden made lookup_concept answer
        ; null, so the s185 rescue for a bare `Some(5)` ran, the payload-type arm
        ; answered on "Rec", and the rescue returned a variant construction of
        ; the OUTER union — same tag, only the first argument as its payload,
        ; rc 0 and not a word (2026-08-16: 34 SIGSEGVs in Array::get_length,
        ; three subsystems from the cause). The rescue standing in FRONT of the
        ; not-found machinery is what swallowed the `use` hint that
        ; xfail_use_vis_hidden.scaly proves is otherwise given.
        ;
        ; So the rescue asks this narrower question and an invisible record falls
        ; through into the existing hard diagnostic — no new message needed.
        ; lookup_variant_tag# itself is unchanged, so `choose` keeps matching by
        ; the payload type as it must; and the three tests that
        ; cover the rescue (definitions__option-some, result-ok, result-err) all
        ; match on the FIELD name and are the refutation control.
        procedure lookup_variant_tag_by_name(
            mutable this: Planner,
            union_name: String,
            variant_name: String
        ) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns int linked

        ; lookup_variant_type: fetch the payload PlannedType for a named
        ; variant. Returns null when the concept isn't a union, the
        ; variant doesn't exist, or the variant has no payload (tag-only
        ; variants like Option.None).
        procedure lookup_variant_type(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable union_name: String,
            variant_name: String
        ) @deep true returns ref[PlannedType]? io linked

        ; match_type_pattern: walk `pattern` and `actual` in parallel,
        ; collecting generic-parameter → concrete-type bindings.
        ; Recursion handles nested generics (e.g. matching `Vector[T]`
        ; against `Vector[int]` yields `T -> int`).
        ;
        ; Runs purely — returns a fresh Vector each call rather than
        ; mutating a caller-supplied accumulator, because passing a
        ; mutable Array as a parameter doesn't propagate in Scaly's
        ; value-semantics model.
        function match_type_pattern(
            this: Planner,
            pattern: PlannedType,
            actual: PlannedType
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true @deep true returns Vector[KeyValuePair[String, PlannedType]] linked

        ; infer_type_arguments: given a function's parameter types
        ; (possibly containing generic parameters like T, K) and the
        ; actual argument types at a call site, infer the concrete type
        ; assignments. Returns a flat Vector of name→type pairs; may
        ; contain duplicates for a generic parameter inferred from
        ; multiple positions (all should agree — agreement is checked
        ; downstream during substitution).
        function infer_type_arguments(
            this: Planner,
            param_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType]
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true @deep true returns Vector[KeyValuePair[String, PlannedType]] linked

        ; instantiate_generic_function: monomorphise a generic function
        ; (one whose parameter types reference type-parameter names like
        ; T / K / V) against a substitution mapping those names to
        ; concrete types. The resulting PlannedFunction has each use of
        ; T replaced with its concrete binding, and is recorded in the
        ; instantiation cache via plan_function.
        procedure instantiate_generic_function(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable f: Function,
            args: Vector[KeyValuePair[String, PlannedType]]
        ) @page true @direct true @stores true @deep true @deep_this_4 true returns PlannedFunction io linked

        ; ================================================================
        ; instantiate_generic: monomorphise a generic Concept against a
        ; concrete type-argument list. Binds each GenericParameter name
        ; to its corresponding argument in `type_substitutions`, plans
        ; the concept's definition so the struct-layout cache gets the
        ; instantiated version, then restores the substitution stack.
        ;
        ; Returns the resulting PlannedType with `generics` populated
        ; and `mangled_name` set via mangle_structure. Missing concepts
        ; fall back to a fresh type variable — keeps the planner
        ; productive on incomplete programs.
        ; ================================================================
        procedure instantiate_generic(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable concept_name: String,
            mutable args: Vector[PlannedType],
            loc: Span
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true @deep true @deep_this_4 true returns PlannedType io linked

        ; ================================================================
        ; check_function_match: test a single Function candidate against
        ; argument types. Returns a ref to the return type if it matches,
        ; null otherwise. `exact_only` selects types_equal (true) vs
        ; types_compatible (false) for parameter/argument comparison.
        ; 'this' parameters are skipped implicitly.
        ; ================================================================
        ; Pure match predicate for overload resolution passes 1 (exact) and
        ; 2 (compatible). NON-`#` on purpose: a #-subcall like plan_type#
        ; allocates on THIS function's local page (not the caller's rp), so
        ; the candidate parameter types planned only to compare against the
        ; args are reclaimed when this returns — instead of accumulating on
        ; the persistent region for every losing candidate of every call
        ; (the dominant transient in -c, measured ~1.2-1.9 MB/LOC of planner
        ; allocation). resolve_function_call# rebuilds the WINNER on rp via
        ; check_function_match#; only the winner's types persist. Mirrors
        ; check_function_match#'s arity + per-param comparison exactly so the
        ; rebuild always agrees. plan_type# is side-effect-free (no
        ; instantiation — pass 3 keeps using check_function_match# directly).
        ; candidate_matches_string_i8: a String argument prefers a
        ; pointer[i8] parameter — the pre-feature representation of a string
        ; literal (before string literals became real String values). Matches
        ; only when EVERY argument is either exactly the parameter type OR a
        ; String against a pointer[i8] parameter, AND at least one String→i8
        ; substitution occurs. Used as a resolution tie-break between the
        ; exact pass (which still picks a real String-parameter overload like
        ; `append(String)`) and the general compatible pass (which would let a
        ; namespace member like Console.print(pointer[const_char]) win for a
        ; bare `print("x")` and then free-mangle to a phantom symbol).
        procedure candidate_matches_string_i8(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable f: Function,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType]
        ) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool io linked

        ; type_is_i8_pointer: pointer[i8] specifically (the canonical string
        ; literal target). Distinct from type_is_cstr_pointer, which also
        ; accepts const_char/char/u8.
        function type_is_i8_pointer(t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        procedure candidate_matches(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable f: Function,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            exact_only: bool
        ) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool io linked

        ; candidate_matches_char_fold: pure predicate for the char-fold
        ; fallback pass. Arity + per-param, where a param that fails the
        ; compatible check still matches when it is char/u8 and the argument
        ; is a foldable String constant. Side-effect-free like candidate_matches
        ; (plan_type# only) — the winner is rebuilt by check_function_match#
        ; with char_fold=true, which folds the operand at the call site.
        procedure candidate_matches_char_fold(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable f: Function,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            arg_ops: Vector[PlannedOperand]
        ) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool io linked

        ; Phase-5 class 1: a persist into a receiver that is itself a
        ; frame-local, non-escaping binding does not outlive the frame —
        ; receiver and argument share the frame's lifetime, so promoting
        ; the argument (or hard-erroring on a missing rp) would be a false
        ; positive. The Planner-instance test code hits exactly this shape
        ; (a local Planner collecting constraints built from local-page
        ; types). `this`, parameters, and escaping/promoted locals all
        ; outlive the frame -> not frame-local.
        function persist_receiver_is_frame_local(this: Planner, is_method: bool, recv: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; Phase-5 class 1: memoized persist summary. Key = the callee's
        ; mangled name; value = function_persists_param_guts over the model
        ; body, computed once. Only consulted when a call argument carries
        ; Local life (see the resolution sites), so the walk runs rarely.
        procedure persists_args_for(mutable this: Planner, mangled: String, f: Function) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns bool linked

        ; Phase-5 class 1 gate: does any argument type carry Local life —
        ; the Design-B local-flip stamp on a call result whose guts live on
        ; THIS frame's local page? Only such calls can need the persist
        ; promotion; sigiled (`#`) arguments carry Call life, so the gate
        ; keeps the summary walk off every sigiled call site (neutrality).
        ; the deep gate's mask for f (which input positions persist), all-ones
        ; when the gate is off or the function is not in the pre-pass
        function persists_mask_for(this: Planner, f: Function) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns u64 linked

        ; does an argument at a PERSISTING position carry Local life
        ; SCALYC_GATE_TRACE: which argument the inline half judged local
        procedure inline_gate_trace(fn_name: String, args: ref[Vector[PlannedOperand]]) @deep true linked

